consiSt("ia",{"courts":[{"l":"HIGHEST COURT","n":"Iowa Supreme Court","d":"Every appeal is filed in the Supreme Court. It keeps cases raising substantial constitutional questions, conflicts, first-impression issues, urgent issues of broad public importance, and lawyer discipline, and transfers the rest to the Court of Appeals. It ordinarily decides cases en banc. It reviews Court of Appeals decisions only on a discretionary application for further review. It also answers certified questions of Iowa law and regulates admission and discipline of lawyers (Iowa Court Rules chapters 31–45)."},{"l":"INTERMEDIATE","n":"Iowa Court of Appeals","d":"Decides only the cases the Supreme Court transfers to it, which may sit in panels. It decides which of its opinions to publish. Unpublished opinions may be cited as persuasive but are not controlling."},{"l":"TRIAL COURTS","n":"Iowa District Court","d":"The single general-jurisdiction trial court, organized into eight judicial districts. District judges, district associate judges, associate juvenile judges, and associate probate judges hear civil, criminal, juvenile, probate, and small-claims matters. Small claims are appealed to a district judge, not to the appellate courts (Iowa R. App. P. 6.105)."},{"l":"SPECIALIZED","n":"Iowa Business Court","d":"A business court created by statute (Iowa Code § 602.6801, Senate File 639). Its judges were appointed effective September 1, 2026, and visiting judges may be assigned. It follows an earlier Iowa Business Specialty Court that operated under supreme court memoranda of operation. Eligibility and transfer rules were not collected; check the statute and current orders."}],"slapp":[{"t":"Iowa adopted the Uniform Public Expression Protection Act in 2025","d":"Iowa Code chapter 652 (2025 Iowa Acts ch. 93) applies to civil actions filed on or after July 1, 2025. It covers claims based on: (a) communications in a legislative, executive, judicial, administrative, or other governmental proceeding; (b) communications on an issue under consideration or review in such a proceeding; or (c) exercise of the federal or Iowa rights of free speech, press, assembly, petition, or association on a matter of public concern. The act must be construed broadly to protect those rights.","c":"Iowa Code §§ 652.1, 652.2(2), 652.11; 2025 Iowa Acts ch. 93, § 13","v":true},{"t":"Only three exclusions","d":"The act does not apply to claims (a) against a governmental unit, or its employee or agent acting or purporting to act officially; (b) by a governmental unit or its official enforcing a law against an imminent threat to public health or safety; or (c) against a person primarily engaged in selling or leasing goods or services, where the claim arises from a communication related to that sale or lease. ‘Goods or services’ excludes creating, disseminating, exhibiting, or advertising dramatic, literary, musical, political, journalistic, or artistic works. The collected text lists no other exclusions.","c":"Iowa Code § 652.2(1), (3)","v":true},{"t":"Procedure: special motion within 60 days; automatic stay","d":"File a special motion for expedited relief within 60 days after being served with the pleading asserting the claim, or later for good cause. Filing stays all other proceedings between the moving and responding parties, including discovery, until the ruling and the time to appeal it expire. The court may allow limited, specific discovery and may hear unrelated motions or emergency injunction motions.\nThe hearing must be held within 60 days after the motion is filed, or within 60 days after an order allowing discovery, unless the court orders a later hearing for good cause. The ruling is due within 60 days after the hearing. A voluntary dismissal does not end the stay’s purpose: the movant can still get a ruling and fees.","c":"Iowa Code §§ 652.3, 652.4, 652.5, 652.8","v":true},{"t":"Standard: three-step test, dismissal with prejudice","d":"The court considers the pleadings, the motion, any reply or response, and evidence that could be considered on summary judgment under Iowa R. Civ. P. 1.981. It must dismiss with prejudice if: (1) the movant shows the act applies; (2) the responding party fails to show an exclusion applies; and (3) either the responding party fails to establish a prima facie case on each essential element, or the movant shows failure to state a claim, or no genuine issue of material fact. A voluntary dismissal with prejudice means the movant prevailed for fee purposes.","c":"Iowa Code §§ 652.6, 652.7","v":true},{"t":"Fees are mandatory","d":"The court shall award court costs, reasonable attorney fees, and reasonable litigation expenses related to the motion to a moving party who prevails. A responding party gets them only if it prevails and the court finds the motion frivolous or filed solely to delay. A fee motion is not subject to the stay.","c":"Iowa Code §§ 652.4(5), 652.10","v":true},{"t":"Immediate appeal within 30 days, with a stay","d":"A moving party may appeal as of right from an order denying the motion in whole or in part, within 30 days after entry. While the appeal is pending, all proceedings between all parties are stayed. Under the appellate rules, an appeal authorized by statute is taken by notice of appeal that names the statute.","c":"Iowa Code §§ 652.4(3), 652.9; Iowa R. App. P. 6.108","v":true}],"oos":[{"t":"Pro hac vice requires Iowa co-counsel who actively participates","d":"A court may admit an eligible out-of-state lawyer for a particular proceeding only if the lawyer appears with an Iowa lawyer. The Iowa lawyer must appear of record, actively participate, accept service for the out-of-state lawyer (Iowa Code § 602.10111), and tell the client of any disagreement in judgment. Agency proceedings also require pro hac vice admission, even where nonlawyers may appear. Admission covers only that proceeding: a separate admission is needed for each later district or appellate court proceeding.","c":"Iowa Ct. R. 31.14(2)–(3)","v":true},{"t":"$250 registration fee, good for 5 years","d":"Before applying, the out-of-state lawyer must register with the Office of Professional Regulation and pay $250 to the client security trust fund. Registration lets the lawyer file pro hac vice applications in any Iowa court or agency for 5 years from the registration date; after that, register and pay again.","c":"Iowa Ct. R. 31.14(5)(r), 31.14(11)","v":true},{"t":"Verified application, served on all parties; five-case limit","d":"The verified application (Form 1 for district court, Form 2 for the supreme court) must be served on all parties who have appeared, with proof of service. It must disclose, among other things, any discipline or contempt and sanctions in the last 5 years, every Iowa pro hac vice application in the last 2 years, and the sponsoring Iowa lawyer’s name and personal identification number.\nThe application ordinarily should be granted. But a lawyer who has appeared pro hac vice in five Iowa proceedings within the preceding two years must show good cause. Any party may object by verified affidavit or later move to revoke.","c":"Iowa Ct. R. 31.14(4)–(8), 31.25","v":true},{"t":"E-filing: the Iowa lawyer files the application; access is case-limited","d":"Under the electronic rules, the Iowa lawyer appearing with the out-of-state lawyer must file the pro hac vice application. Only after it is granted does the out-of-state lawyer register for EDMS and enter an appearance. E-filing is then mandatory for pro hac vice counsel, at trial and on appeal. Pro hac vice lawyers can view remotely only the documents in the cases where they are admitted.","c":"Iowa R. Elec. P. 16.302(1), 16.304(1)(b)(1), 16.502(1)(b); Iowa R. App. P. 6.1(1)","v":true},{"t":"Appearing without admission is unauthorized practice","d":"An out-of-state lawyer who appears before an Iowa court, or before an agency where practice is limited to lawyers, without pro hac vice admission is engaged in the unauthorized practice of law. Work reasonably related to a proceeding in which the lawyer reasonably expects to be admitted is permitted. The lawyer submits to Iowa discipline from the moment the application is filed.","c":"Iowa Ct. R. 31.14(9); Iowa R. of Prof’l Conduct 32:5.5(c)","v":true},{"t":"Limited-scope appearances are allowed by notice","d":"A lawyer may appear for one or more specific proceedings by filing and serving a notice of limited appearance before or at the proceeding. The lawyer must tell the court of the limit at the start of any hearing, and exits without leave by filing a notice of completion of limited appearance. Service on a limited-appearance lawyer is valid only for those proceedings.\nPapers ghostwritten for a self-represented party must say so and list the drafting lawyer’s name and identification number, but the lawyer does not sign them. The client’s consent to limited scope generally must be confirmed in writing.","c":"Iowa R. Civ. P. 1.404(3)–(4), 1.423, 1.442(2); Iowa R. Elec. P. 16.320; Iowa R. of Prof’l Conduct 32:1.2(c)","v":true},{"t":"Admission on motion: 5 of the last 7 years","d":"A lawyer licensed elsewhere may be admitted without examination after regularly practicing (at least 1,000 hours a year) for 5 of the 7 years before applying. Apply through the NCBE character and fitness process, paying a nonrefundable $900 fee to the Office of Professional Regulation plus the NCBE investigation fee. Lawyers who failed an Iowa bar exam within 5 years, or failed five or more bar exams, are ineligible. An application not granted within one year is deemed withdrawn.","c":"Iowa Ct. R. 31.12","v":true}],"ethics":[{"t":"Governing code","d":"The Iowa Rules of Professional Conduct are Iowa Court Rules chapter 32, cited ‘Iowa R. of Prof’l Conduct 32:x.x.’ The collected chapter is headed December 2020; it was not amended in the 2025–2026 orders listed by the Iowa Judicial Branch. The rules track the ABA Model Rules, with the Iowa differences below. Trust accounts are governed by chapter 45, and chapter 33 sets out Standards for Professional Conduct.","c":"Iowa R. of Prof’l Conduct 32:1.0–32:8.5; Iowa Ct. R. chs. 33, 45","v":true},{"t":"Mandatory disclosure to prevent imminent death or serious harm","d":"In addition to the ABA-style permissive exceptions, Iowa requires a lawyer to reveal client information to the extent the lawyer reasonably believes necessary to prevent imminent death or substantial bodily harm.","c":"Iowa R. of Prof’l Conduct 32:1.6(c)","v":true},{"t":"Sexual relations and related-lawyer conflicts","d":"The ban on sexual relations reaches a client or a client’s representative. Exceptions: a spouse, or a relationship that predates the representation. Even then, the lawyer should withdraw if the representation may be impaired. Iowa also keeps a ‘related lawyer’ rule. A lawyer may not oppose a person represented by a parent, child, sibling, spouse, cohabiting partner, or other familial or romantic relation without the client’s informed consent confirmed in a writing signed by the client.","c":"Iowa R. of Prof’l Conduct 32:1.8(j), (l)","v":true},{"t":"Trust records kept 6 years; limited scope in writing","d":"Trust account records must be preserved for six years after the representation ends, and chapter 45 governs client trust accounts. A limited-scope agreement must generally be confirmed in writing, with exceptions for telephone-only advice, nonprofit or court-annexed programs, and limited court appointments. A signed writing creates a presumption that the representation is limited to what it describes. A person receiving limited-scope help is treated as unrepresented for the no-contact rule, unless opposing counsel has written notice to communicate with the limited-scope lawyer.","c":"Iowa R. of Prof’l Conduct 32:1.15(a), (f), 32:1.2(c), 32:4.2(b)","v":true},{"t":"Trial publicity: presumption-of-innocence statement","d":"Any permitted public statement that a defendant will be or has been charged with a crime must also explain that a charge is merely an accusation and the defendant is presumed innocent until proven guilty.","c":"Iowa R. of Prof’l Conduct 32:3.6(e)","v":true},{"t":"Reporting and discrimination rules","d":"A lawyer who knows another lawyer violated the Iowa rules must report it. The text has no ‘substantial question as to honesty, trustworthiness or fitness’ qualifier. Information protected by Rule 32:1.6, Iowa Code § 622.10, or an approved lawyers assistance program is exempt. It is misconduct to engage in sexual harassment or other unlawful discrimination in the practice of law, or to knowingly permit staff or agents to do so.","c":"Iowa R. of Prof’l Conduct 32:8.3, 32:8.4(g)","v":true}],"style":"State of Iowa","home":"https://www.iowacourts.gov/","unique":{"mines":[{"t":"No default without a 10-day mailed notice of intent","d":"Before applying for a default, mail written notice of intent to the defaulting party and to any lawyer you know represents it, at least 10 days before filing the application. The days run from mailing. The application must certify the notice and attach a copy (Form 10). Exceptions: small claims, eviction (forcible entry and detainer), juvenile cases, and service by publication.","c":"Iowa R. Civ. P. 1.972(2)–(4)"},{"t":"Do not plead a dollar amount; say ‘law’ or ‘equity’","d":"Except in small claims and liquidated-damages cases, a pleading may not state the specific amount of money damages sought. It states only whether the amount meets jurisdictional requirements. The petition must also state whether the action is at law or in equity, which affects trial and appellate review (equity cases are reviewed de novo).","c":"Iowa R. Civ. P. 1.403(1)–(2); Iowa R. App. P. 6.907"},{"t":"Post-trial motions are due 15 days after the decision is filed","d":"Not 28 days after judgment as in federal court. Motions for new trial and JNOV, and a Rule 1.904(2) motion to amend findings, are due 15 days after the verdict, report, or decision is filed. The court may extend by at most 30 days, for good cause and not ex parte. Only a timely motion restarts the appeal clock, and a second such motion by the same party does not.","c":"Iowa R. Civ. P. 1.904(2), 1.1007; Iowa R. App. P. 6.101(1)(b)–(c)"},{"t":"Appeal: 30 days, filed in the district court, then 7-day follow-ups","d":"The notice of appeal goes to the district court clerk, with an informational copy to the supreme court, within 30 days after the judgment is filed. The appellate courts cannot extend this. Within 7 more days you must pay the $150 fee and file and serve the combined certificate ordering the transcript.","c":"Iowa R. App. P. 6.101(1)(b), 6.102(3), 6.804(1), 6.1003(1)"},{"t":"Further review: 20 days, and rehearing does not stop the clock","d":"An application for supreme court review of a Court of Appeals decision is due 20 days after the decision is filed, and the supreme court cannot extend it. A petition for rehearing in the Court of Appeals (due in 7 days) does not toll the 20 days, so file both if needed.","c":"Iowa R. App. P. 6.1003(1)(e), 6.1103(1)(a), 6.1204(1)–(2)"},{"t":"Preserve error and say how in the brief","d":"Each argument must open with a statement of how the issue was preserved, with record cites to where it was raised and decided below. Citing the notice of appeal is not enough. Uncited issues may be deemed waived. Each missed appellate deadline brings a $150 penalty that the lawyer pays personally.","c":"Iowa R. App. P. 6.903(2)(a)(8), 6.1202(2)"},{"t":"Serve within 90 days of filing","d":"If the original notice is not served within 90 days after the petition is filed, the court must dismiss without prejudice as to that defendant, or direct another time or manner of service, unless good cause is shown.","c":"Iowa R. Civ. P. 1.302(5)"},{"t":"Old cases are swept every year","d":"A case whose petition was filed more than one year before July 15 must be tried before January 1 of the next year, or it is dismissed without prejudice at the plaintiff’s cost. The clerk sends notice by August 15. A continuance requires a court order; a stipulation alone does not work. Reinstatement must be sought within six months.","c":"Iowa R. Civ. P. 1.944(2), (5)–(6)"},{"t":"$1,000 late-settlement fee","d":"Waiving a jury or settling later than two full working days before a scheduled jury trial, or settling during trial, brings a $1,000 fee assessed as court costs. The court may not waive it or grant a continuance to avoid it.","c":"Iowa R. Civ. P. 1.909"},{"t":"Only 60 days to set aside a default judgment","d":"A motion to set aside a default or default judgment for mistake, inadvertence, surprise, excusable neglect, or unavoidable casualty must be filed promptly after the grounds are discovered, and never more than 60 days after entry of the judgment.","c":"Iowa R. Civ. P. 1.977"},{"t":"Discovery conference within 21 days of the first answer, even with a motion pending","d":"The parties must confer no later than 21 days after any defendant answers or appears. A pending pre-answer motion does not suspend the conference or initial disclosures.","c":"Iowa R. Civ. P. 1.507(1)"},{"t":"Pro hac vice is case-by-case and filed by Iowa counsel","d":"Register and pay $250 with the Office of Professional Regulation first. Your Iowa co-counsel files the application electronically, and you can e-file only after it is granted. Admission does not carry over to the appeal: apply again in each later district or appellate proceeding. After five admissions in two years, you must show good cause.","c":"Iowa Ct. R. 31.14(2)(c), 31.14(7)(d), 31.14(11); Iowa R. Elec. P. 16.304(1)(b)(1)"}],"judge":[{"t":"No peremptory change of judge in civil cases","d":"The collected Iowa Rules of Civil Procedure and Rules of Appellate Procedure contain no procedure for removing a judge without cause. Seek disqualification for cause under the Code of Judicial Conduct.","c":"Iowa R. Civ. P. ch. 1; Iowa R. App. P. ch. 6 (searched)","v":true},{"t":"Disqualification for cause under the Code of Judicial Conduct","d":"A judge must step aside whenever the judge’s impartiality might reasonably be questioned. Listed grounds include personal bias or knowledge of disputed facts; a relative within the third degree as party, lawyer, interested person, or likely material witness; family economic interests; prior involvement as lawyer, government lawyer, witness, or judge in another court; and public statements committing the judge to a result.\nIowa adds a due-process ground: campaign contributions, or independent expenditures, by donors associated with a party or counsel, when the judge learns of them from mandated disclosures or a timely motion. Except for bias, the parties and lawyers may agree, outside the judge’s presence, to waive disqualification, and the agreement goes on the record.","c":"Iowa Code of Judicial Conduct 51:2.11(A), (C)","v":true}],"speech":[{"t":"Anti-SLAPP act read broadly","d":"Chapter 652 must be broadly construed and applied to protect speech, press, assembly, petition, and association, and applied uniformly with other states that enact the uniform act.","c":"Iowa Code §§ 652.11, 652.12","v":true},{"t":"Cameras in court: allowed with authorization; object 3 days ahead","d":"Expanded news media coverage is permitted with the judicial officer’s prior authorization, unless it would materially interfere with a fair trial. Requests go through the media coordinator, who gives at least 7 days’ notice. A party must file a written objection at least 3 days before the proceeding, and lawyers must tell their witnesses of their right to object.\nCoverage of jury selection is prohibited. Coverage is also barred in juvenile, dissolution, adoption, custody, and trade-secret cases unless all parties consent on the record.","c":"Iowa Ct. R. 25.2, 25.3(2)–(3)","v":true}],"other":[{"t":"Expedited civil actions up to $75,000","d":"A plaintiff whose only claim is for money and whose claims against any one party total $75,000 or less may elect the expedited track. The election caps recovery at that amount and brings streamlined procedures: discovery completed 60 days before trial, trial within one year of filing, a 6-person jury (3 strikes per side; 5 of 6 may decide after 3 hours of deliberation), and no more than 6 hours per side for jury selection and presentation.","c":"Iowa R. Civ. P. 1.281","v":true},{"t":"Motion practice","d":"Unless a rule or order says otherwise, a resistance is due 10 days after service of a motion. A reply is due 7 days after the resistance, or before the hearing if earlier, and is limited to new authority or unanticipated matters. Legal briefs are served (with a copy delivered to the presiding judge) but not filed, except on summary judgment or by order; file a certificate of compliance. The court must rule within 30 days of submission unless it extends the time on the record.","c":"Iowa R. Civ. P. 1.431(4)–(7), 1.442(4)","v":true},{"t":"Redact protected information","d":"Do not put Social Security numbers, financial account numbers, dates of birth, names of minor children, taxpayer or personal identification numbers, or other unique identifiers in public filings. Omit them, use only a portion (such as initials or the birth year), or put them on the separate protected information form. Sanctions apply.","c":"Iowa R. Elec. P. 16.602–16.606, 16.609","v":true}]},"appeals":{"asof":"November 10, 2026","src":"Iowa Rules of Appellate Procedure, Iowa Court Rules chapter 6 (chapter pages headed December 2025), and chapter 21 (organization of the appellate courts, March 2013), from the Iowa Legislature’s official Iowa Court Rules publication of October 30, 2026 (legis.iowa.gov); Iowa Rules of Civil Procedure 1.904, 1.1007 (chapter 1, July 2023); Iowa Rules of Electronic Procedure (chapter 16, March 2025); Iowa Code chapter 652 (Iowa Code 2026). A September 28, 2026 supreme court order amends chapter 6 rules on juvenile (chapter 232) appeals effective January 1, 2027; that amendment is not reflected here.","intro":{"t":"Appeals in Iowa: one notice to the supreme court, 30 days, no extensions","d":"Every Iowa appeal is filed in the Iowa Supreme Court, even though most cases are decided by the Court of Appeals. You file the notice of appeal with the clerk of the district court within 30 days after the final order or judgment is filed, and file an informational copy with the supreme court. The supreme court then keeps the case or transfers it to the Court of Appeals under published criteria. A party unhappy with a Court of Appeals decision has only 20 days to apply to the supreme court for further review, and that time cannot be extended.\nThe appellate courts cannot extend the deadlines to start a case (notice of appeal, interlocutory application, discretionary review, certiorari, further review). The only exception is when the district court clerk failed to give notice of the ruling. All other deadlines can be extended for good cause, but a missed deadline triggers a default notice and a $150 penalty that the lawyer pays personally."},"groups":[["Starting the appeal",[{"t":"Notice of appeal: 30 days from filing of the final order or judgment","d":"File the notice with the clerk of the district court where the order was entered, and file an informational copy with the clerk of the supreme court, within 30 days after the final order or judgment is filed. In juvenile (Iowa Code chapter 232) child-in-need-of-assistance and termination cases the time is 15 days, and the client must sign the notice too.\nThe notice must name the parties appealing and the order appealed from, substantially follow Form 1, be served on any court reporter who reported a proceeding involved, and be served on the attorney general if the State is a party.","c":"Iowa R. App. P. 6.101(1)(a)–(b), 6.102(1)–(2)"},{"t":"Fee and combined certificate within 7 days","d":"Within 7 days after filing the notice of appeal, pay the $150 filing fee to the clerk of the supreme court (or move to waive or defer it). Within the same 7 days, file the combined certificate (Form 2) with both clerks and serve it on each court reporter. In it you certify that you ordered the transcript, say which parts you ordered and the issues you plan to raise, and state whether expedited deadlines apply.","c":"Iowa R. App. P. 6.102(3), 6.703(1)(a), 6.804(1)–(4)"},{"t":"Deadlines to start a case cannot be extended","d":"The supreme court may not extend the deadline for a notice of appeal, an interlocutory application, an application for discretionary review, or a certiorari petition. The one exception is when the district court clerk failed to notify the party of the ruling. Then a motion must be filed within 60 days after the original deadline expired, supported by affidavits, and any extension runs no more than 30 days from the order granting it. A technical failure of the e-filing system does not excuse a jurisdictional deadline either.","c":"Iowa R. App. P. 6.101(5), 6.1003(1); Iowa R. Elec. P. 16.309(2)(a)"},{"t":"Safety valves: early notice, service, wrong form","d":"A notice filed before the order is filed is timely if the order is filed within 30 days after the notice. The time to file a notice of appeal is tolled when the notice is served, if it is filed within a reasonable time. A case started by the wrong form of review (appeal instead of certiorari, for example) is not dismissed; it proceeds as if the proper form had been used. That rule does not extend any filing time.","c":"Iowa R. App. P. 6.101(3)–(4), 6.151"},{"t":"What is final and when to appeal partial rulings","d":"Final orders and judgments are appealable as of right. An order granting or denying a new trial is final. An order setting aside a default is final only in dissolution or annulment cases. An order disposing of some but not all parties or issues may be appealed within the time for appealing the judgment that finally disposes of everything, even if the claims are severable. A post-judgment attorney-fee ruling is separately appealable; the district court keeps jurisdiction to decide fees despite the appeal.","c":"Iowa R. App. P. 6.101(1)(d), 6.103(1), 6.103(3)"}]],["Tolling and post-decision motions",[{"t":"Rule 1.904(2) and post-trial motions restart the 30 days","d":"If a motion under Iowa R. Civ. P. 1.904(2) (to reconsider, enlarge, or amend) or 1.1007 (new trial, judgment notwithstanding the verdict) is timely filed, the notice of appeal is due 30 days after the ruling on that motion. A motion counts as timely if it is filed by the deadline and asks the court to reconsider, enlarge, or amend; whether it was ‘proper’ does not matter. A second such motion by the same party does not count, unless the court modified its ruling and the new motion addresses only the modification.","c":"Iowa R. App. P. 6.101(1)(b)–(c) and comment"},{"t":"Post-trial motions are due in 15 days","d":"Motions for new trial and for judgment notwithstanding the verdict must be filed within 15 days after the verdict, report, or decision is filed with the clerk. The court may grant up to 30 more days, for good cause and not ex parte. A Rule 1.904(2) motion must be joined with, or filed within the time for, a new-trial motion.","c":"Iowa R. Civ. P. 1.904(2), 1.1007"}]],["Cross-appeals",[{"t":"Cross-appeal: the 30-day limit or 10 days after the notice, whichever is later","d":"File the notice of cross-appeal in the district court, with an informational copy to the supreme court, within the 30-day appeal period or within 10 days after the notice of appeal was filed, whichever is later. In chapter 232 juvenile cases the base period is 15 days. A cross-appeal also requires the $150 fee.","c":"Iowa R. App. P. 6.101(2), 6.703(1)(a)"},{"t":"Briefing with a cross-appeal","d":"The appellee may file a combined appellee/cross-appellant brief. The appellant/cross-appellee must then respond within 21 days, by reply brief or by a statement waiving further briefing. The appellee/cross-appellant may reply to that within 14 days.","c":"Iowa R. App. P. 6.901(1)(b)–(c), 6.903(5)–(6)"}]],["Record and transcripts",[{"t":"Transcript deadlines run from the combined certificate","d":"The court reporter must file the transcript within 40 days after service of the combined certificate in ordinary cases (20 days for guilty pleas and sentencing; 30 days in chapter 232 cases). The clerk of the supreme court sends a briefing-deadline notice once all ordered transcripts are filed. That notice starts the appellant’s brief clock.","c":"Iowa R. App. P. 6.803, 6.803(6)"},{"t":"No appendix: attach the rulings to the brief","d":"The appendix process was eliminated. The orders and judgments appealed from are attached to the appellant’s brief instead. In exceptional cases a party may ask leave to file an appendix.","c":"Iowa R. App. P. 6.903(2)(b); comment to reserved rule 6.905"}]],["Briefs",[{"t":"Schedule: 50 / 30 / 21 days","d":"Appellant’s brief: 50 days after the clerk’s briefing-deadline notice. Appellee’s brief (or a statement waiving it): 30 days after service of the appellant’s brief. Appellant’s reply: 21 days after service of the appellee’s brief. Each brief may be amended once without leave: the opening brief within 15 days after service, the appellee’s within 10 days, and a reply within 7 days.","c":"Iowa R. App. P. 6.901(1), 6.901(8)"},{"t":"Expedited cases: half the time, no extensions","d":"Custody, physical care and visitation, adoption, chapter 600A and chapter 232 termination and CINA cases, guilty-plea and sentencing-only criminal appeals, juvenile placement, lawyer discipline, involuntary commitments, and certified questions are expedited. Briefing times (other than replies) are cut in half. Replies are due 15 days after the appellee’s brief (7 days for a cross-appellant’s reply). The court will not extend expedited deadlines.","c":"Iowa R. App. P. 6.902"},{"t":"Length and typeface","d":"A brief may contain up to 13,000 words in a proportionally spaced typeface (14-point or larger, with serifs, including footnotes), or 1,300 lines in a monospaced typeface. A reply brief gets half that. A handwritten brief is limited to 50 pages, or 25 for a reply. Headings, footnotes, and quotations count. File the Form 7 certificate of compliance. Page numbers go bottom center, starting with the cover as page one, with no Roman numerals.","c":"Iowa R. App. P. 6.903(1)(f)–(i)"},{"t":"Required contents: routing statement and error preservation","d":"The appellant’s brief must include a routing statement saying whether the supreme court should retain the case or transfer it to the Court of Appeals under Rule 6.1101. Each argument division must begin by stating how the issue was preserved, with record cites showing where it was raised and decided below; citing the notice of appeal does not satisfy this. Next comes the scope and standard of review. Failure to cite authority may waive an issue, and nothing may be incorporated by reference.","c":"Iowa R. App. P. 6.903(2)(a)(4), 6.903(2)(a)(8)"},{"t":"Citations, including unpublished opinions","d":"Cite Iowa cases to the North Western Reporter with a pin cite. Unpublished opinions are not controlling authority but may be cited as persuasive. Give the docket number and an electronic-database cite; if the opinion is not in a readily accessible database, you must file and serve a copy.","c":"Iowa R. App. P. 6.904(2)(a)"}]],["Stays, bonds, and motions",[{"t":"Supersedeas bond","d":"An appeal does not stay the judgment unless a supersedeas bond is filed and approved in the district court, or the court orders a stay. For a money judgment the bond may not exceed 110% of the judgment, unless the district court sets it higher under Iowa Code section 625A.9(2)(a), and it is subject to the statutory maximum in section 625A.9(2)(b). In other cases the bond must hold the appellee harmless, and it may not be less than $1,000. Cash, a certificate of deposit, or a government security may be used.","c":"Iowa R. App. P. 6.601(1)–(4)"},{"t":"Motions: 14 days to resist, 3 to reply","d":"A resistance is due within 14 days after service of a motion, and a reply within 3 days after service of the resistance. Attachments are limited to 25 pages unless the court orders otherwise. A party adversely affected by a ruling made without awaiting a resistance may ask for review within 10 days.","c":"Iowa R. App. P. 6.1002"},{"t":"Missed deadlines cost the lawyer $150","d":"When an appellant misses a deadline, the clerk issues a default notice: cure within 15 days or the appeal may be dismissed. An appellee who misses the brief deadline loses oral argument unless the brief is filed within 15 days of the notice. Each default notice sent to a lawyer carries a $150 penalty that the lawyer must pay personally, not charge to the client. A dismissal for a represented appellant’s default is reported to the Attorney Disciplinary Board.","c":"Iowa R. App. P. 6.1202(1)–(3)"},{"t":"Extensions of other deadlines","d":"Nonjurisdictional deadlines may be shortened or extended for good cause, on motion or by the court on its own. Examples of good cause: illness of counsel, unusual and compelling unavailability, a missing transcript, or a real chance of settlement. In expedited cases the motion must say so.","c":"Iowa R. App. P. 6.1003(2)"}]],["Argument, decision, and rehearing",[{"t":"Ask for oral argument in the brief","d":"A request for oral argument must be made in the party’s brief; otherwise argument will ordinarily not be granted. The court denies argument that is unlikely to help it.","c":"Iowa R. App. P. 6.903(2)(a)(10), 6.908(1)–(2)"},{"t":"Scope of review","d":"Review in equity cases is de novo. In all other cases the appellate courts correct errors at law.","c":"Iowa R. App. P. 6.907"},{"t":"Rehearing in the Court of Appeals: 7 days, and it does not stop the further-review clock","d":"File within 7 days after the Court of Appeals decision. A petition not granted or denied within 7 days is deemed denied. No response is received unless requested, and there is no oral argument. The 20-day period for further review keeps running, so a party may file both. The supreme court may stay a pending further-review application while rehearing is pending. The petition is limited to one-fifth of the brief length.","c":"Iowa R. App. P. 6.1204"},{"t":"Rehearing in the supreme court: 14 days","d":"File within 14 days after the supreme court opinion. No response is allowed unless the court asks for one, and the court ordinarily will not grant rehearing without asking. No rehearing may be sought from an order denying further review. The petition is limited to one-fifth of the brief length.","c":"Iowa R. App. P. 6.1205"}]],["Review in the Iowa Supreme Court",[{"t":"Routing: retained or transferred","d":"The supreme court ordinarily keeps cases raising substantial constitutional questions about a statute or rule, conflicts in published decisions, substantial issues of first impression, urgent issues of broad public importance, lawyer discipline, and the announcing or changing of legal principles. Cases that apply existing principles, or that suit summary disposition, ordinarily go to the Court of Appeals.","c":"Iowa R. App. P. 6.1101"},{"t":"Further review: 20 days, discretionary, no extensions","d":"Apply within 20 days after the Court of Appeals decision is filed (10 days in chapter 232 juvenile cases). Neither court may extend this, except that the Court of Appeals must extend it when the clerk failed to notify the party of the decision. Further review is discretionary and ‘will not be granted in normal circumstances.’ Grounds include conflicting decisions, important unsettled questions, changing legal principles, and broad public importance.\nThe application must open with the questions presented, alone on the first page after the cover, followed by a statement supporting review and a brief. It is limited to two-fifths of the brief length, plus the decision and up to 10 pages of record. A resistance is due within 10 days; replies are not allowed. If review is granted, the supreme court may consider any issue raised in the appeal.","c":"Iowa R. App. P. 6.1003(1)(e), 6.1103(1)–(5)"}]],["Interlocutory and special appeals",[{"t":"Interlocutory appeal by permission: 30 days","d":"Apply to the supreme court within 30 days after entry of the ruling, or within 30 days after the ruling on a timely Rule 1.904(2) motion. The court grants permission only if the ruling involves substantial rights and will materially affect the final decision, and if deciding its correctness before trial will better serve the interests of justice. A grant stays the trial court. The fee is $100, plus $50 more if granted. Without an interlocutory appeal, errors in interlocutory rulings are preserved for the appeal from the final judgment.","c":"Iowa R. App. P. 6.103(4), 6.104(1)–(3), 6.703(1)(b)"},{"t":"Certiorari and discretionary review: 30 days","d":"An original certiorari action claiming that a district judge, district associate judge, associate juvenile judge, or associate probate judge exceeded jurisdiction or acted illegally must be filed in the supreme court within 30 days after the decision (or after a ruling on a timely 1.904(2) or 1.1007 motion). Applications for discretionary review of orders that a statute makes reviewable only by permission are also due in 30 days.","c":"Iowa R. App. P. 6.106(1), 6.107(1)"},{"t":"Appeals of right created by statute or rule","d":"When a statute or rule grants an appeal as of right from something other than a final judgment, use a notice of appeal and name the statute or rule that authorizes it. Examples given in the comment: qualified immunity (Iowa Code § 670.4A(4)), arbitration (§ 679A.17), and class certification (Iowa R. Civ. P. 1.264(3)). An order denying, in whole or in part, an anti-SLAPP motion under Iowa’s Uniform Public Expression Protection Act is appealable as of right by the moving party within 30 days after entry, and all proceedings are stayed during that appeal.","c":"Iowa R. App. P. 6.108 and comment; Iowa Code §§ 652.4(3), 652.9"},{"t":"Small claims go to a district judge, not the appellate courts","d":"A case tried as a small claim (except one involving an interest in real estate) cannot be appealed to the supreme court. It is appealed to the district court under Iowa Code section 631.13, and further review is only by discretionary review under section 631.16 and Rule 6.106.","c":"Iowa R. App. P. 6.105"},{"t":"Certified questions","d":"Federal and other courts may certify questions of Iowa law to the supreme court under Iowa Code chapter 684A. Certified-question cases are on the expedited track.","c":"Iowa R. App. P. 6.301–6.305, 6.902(1)(j)"}]],["E-filing and time computation",[{"t":"Electronic filing is mandatory, including for pro hac vice counsel","d":"All Iowa lawyers, all lawyers admitted pro hac vice, and self-represented parties must register for and use the Electronic Document Management System (EDMS) in appellate cases. The Rules of Electronic Procedure, including the privacy and redaction rules, apply on appeal.","c":"Iowa R. App. P. 6.1(1)–(2); Iowa R. Elec. P. 16.302(1)"},{"t":"Filed by midnight; technical trouble is no excuse","d":"A document is timely if filed before midnight on the due date. The electronic file stamp is the official filing time. A technical problem never excuses a jurisdictional deadline. For a nonjurisdictional deadline, file by the soonest available means and ask the court to treat the filing as timely.","c":"Iowa R. Elec. P. 16.309(1)–(2)"}]],["Deadlines",[{"t":"Notice of appeal (final order or judgment)","d":"30 days from filing of the final order or judgment.\nFiled in the district court with an informational copy to the supreme court. Not extendable except for the clerk’s failure to give notice (Rule 6.101(5)).","c":"Iowa R. App. P. 6.101(1)(b)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the final order or judgment)","note":"Filed in the district court with an informational copy to the supreme court. Not extendable except for the clerk’s failure to give notice (Rule 6.101(5))."}},{"t":"Notice of appeal after a timely Rule 1.904(2) or 1.1007 motion","d":"30 days from filing of the ruling on the motion.\nA successive motion by the same party does not count unless it addresses only a modification.","c":"Iowa R. App. P. 6.101(1)(b)–(c)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the ruling on the motion)","note":"A successive motion by the same party does not count unless it addresses only a modification."}},{"t":"Notice of appeal in chapter 232 CINA or termination cases","d":"15 days from filing of the order or judgment.\nAmendments effective January 1, 2027 change juvenile appeal rules; check the new text.","c":"Iowa R. App. P. 6.101(1)(a)","due":{"n":15,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the order or judgment)","note":"Amendments effective January 1, 2027 change juvenile appeal rules; check the new text."}},{"t":"Notice of cross-appeal","d":"30 days from filing of the final order, or 10 days after the notice of appeal, whichever is later.\n15-day base period in chapter 232 cases.","c":"Iowa R. App. P. 6.101(2)(b)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the final order, or 10 days after the notice of appeal, whichever is later)","note":"15-day base period in chapter 232 cases."}},{"t":"Motion to extend appeal time for lack of clerk notice","d":"60 days from expiration of the original appeal deadline.\nAny extension granted runs no more than 30 days after the order granting it.","c":"Iowa R. App. P. 6.101(5)","due":{"n":60,"from":"the trigger date (expiration of the original appeal deadline)","note":"Any extension granted runs no more than 30 days after the order granting it."}},{"t":"Filing fee and combined certificate","d":"7 days from filing of the notice of appeal.\n$150 fee. Certificate to both clerks and to court reporters.","c":"Iowa R. App. P. 6.102(3), 6.804(1)","due":{"n":7,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the notice of appeal)","note":"$150 fee. Certificate to both clerks and to court reporters."}},{"t":"Transcript filed by court reporter","d":"40 days from service of the combined certificate.\n20 days for guilty pleas and sentencing; 30 days in chapter 232 cases.","c":"Iowa R. App. P. 6.803","due":{"n":40,"from":"the trigger date (service of the combined certificate)","note":"20 days for guilty pleas and sentencing; 30 days in chapter 232 cases."}},{"t":"Appellant’s brief","d":"50 days from clerk’s notice of briefing deadline.\nHalved in expedited cases (Rule 6.902).","c":"Iowa R. App. P. 6.901(1)(a)","due":{"n":50,"from":"the trigger date (clerk’s notice of briefing deadline)","note":"Halved in expedited cases (Rule 6.902)."}},{"t":"Appellee’s brief","d":"30 days from service of the appellant’s brief.\nOr a written statement waiving the brief.","c":"Iowa R. App. P. 6.901(1)(b)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (service of the appellant’s brief)","note":"Or a written statement waiving the brief."}},{"t":"Appellant’s reply brief","d":"21 days from service of the appellee’s brief.\nWith a cross-appeal the appellant must reply or file a waiver; the cross-appellant then has 14 days to reply.","c":"Iowa R. App. P. 6.901(1)(c)","due":{"n":21,"from":"the trigger date (service of the appellee’s brief)","note":"With a cross-appeal the appellant must reply or file a waiver; the cross-appellant then has 14 days to reply."}},{"t":"Resistance to a motion","d":"14 days from service of the motion.\nReply within 3 days after the resistance.","c":"Iowa R. App. P. 6.1002(2)","due":{"n":14,"from":"the trigger date (service of the motion)","note":"Reply within 3 days after the resistance."}},{"t":"Cure of default after clerk’s notice","d":"15 days from issuance of the default notice.\n$150 penalty to the lawyer for each default notice.","c":"Iowa R. App. P. 6.1202(1)","due":{"n":15,"from":"the trigger date (issuance of the default notice)","note":"$150 penalty to the lawyer for each default notice."}},{"t":"Application for interlocutory appeal","d":"30 days from entry of the ruling (or of the ruling on a timely 1.904(2) motion).\n15 days in chapter 232 cases.","c":"Iowa R. App. P. 6.104(1)(c)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (entry of the ruling)","note":"15 days in chapter 232 cases."}},{"t":"Petition for writ of certiorari","d":"30 days from entry of the challenged decision.\nRuns from the ruling on a timely 1.904(2) or 1.1007 motion.","c":"Iowa R. App. P. 6.107(1)(b)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (entry of the challenged decision)","note":"Runs from the ruling on a timely 1.904(2) or 1.1007 motion."}},{"t":"Application for discretionary review","d":"30 days from entry of the ruling, order, or judgment.","c":"Iowa R. App. P. 6.106(1)(b)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (entry of the ruling, order, or judgment)","note":""}},{"t":"Petition for rehearing in the Court of Appeals","d":"7 days from filing of the Court of Appeals decision.\nDoes not toll the 20-day further-review period.","c":"Iowa R. App. P. 6.1204(2)","due":{"n":7,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the Court of Appeals decision)","note":"Does not toll the 20-day further-review period."}},{"t":"Application for further review","d":"20 days from filing of the Court of Appeals decision.\n10 days in chapter 232 cases. Not extendable by the supreme court.","c":"Iowa R. App. P. 6.1103(1)(a)(2)","due":{"n":20,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the Court of Appeals decision)","note":"10 days in chapter 232 cases. Not extendable by the supreme court."}},{"t":"Resistance to application for further review","d":"10 days from service of the application.\nNo replies. Not permitted in chapter 232 cases unless requested.","c":"Iowa R. App. P. 6.1103(2)(b)","due":{"n":10,"from":"the trigger date (service of the application)","note":"No replies. Not permitted in chapter 232 cases unless requested."}},{"t":"Petition for rehearing in the supreme court","d":"14 days from filing of the supreme court opinion.\nUnless the court shortens or enlarges the time.","c":"Iowa R. App. P. 6.1205(1)","due":{"n":14,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the supreme court opinion)","note":"Unless the court shortens or enlarges the time."}},{"t":"Appeal from denial of anti-SLAPP motion","d":"30 days from entry of the order.\nMoving party only; proceedings are stayed during the appeal (§ 652.4(3)).","c":"Iowa Code § 652.9","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (entry of the order)","note":"Moving party only; proceedings are stayed during the appeal (§ 652.4(3))."}},{"t":"Motion for new trial or JNOV (trial court)","d":"15 days from filing of the verdict, report, or decision.\nCourt may add up to 30 days for good cause, not ex parte.","c":"Iowa R. Civ. P. 1.1007","due":{"n":15,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the verdict, report, or decision)","note":"Court may add up to 30 days for good cause, not ex parte."}}]]],"dl":[["Notice of appeal (final order or judgment)",30,"Filing of the final order or judgment","Iowa R. App. P. 6.101(1)(b)","Filed in the district court with an informational copy to the supreme court. Not extendable except for the clerk’s failure to give notice (Rule 6.101(5))."],["Notice of appeal after a timely Rule 1.904(2) or 1.1007 motion",30,"Filing of the ruling on the motion","Iowa R. App. P. 6.101(1)(b)–(c)","A successive motion by the same party does not count unless it addresses only a modification."],["Notice of appeal in chapter 232 CINA or termination cases",15,"Filing of the order or judgment","Iowa R. App. P. 6.101(1)(a)","Amendments effective January 1, 2027 change juvenile appeal rules; check the new text."],["Notice of cross-appeal",30,"Filing of the final order, or 10 days after the notice of appeal, whichever is later","Iowa R. App. P. 6.101(2)(b)","15-day base period in chapter 232 cases."],["Motion to extend appeal time for lack of clerk notice",60,"Expiration of the original appeal deadline","Iowa R. App. P. 6.101(5)","Any extension granted runs no more than 30 days after the order granting it."],["Filing fee and combined certificate",7,"Filing of the notice of appeal","Iowa R. App. P. 6.102(3), 6.804(1)","$150 fee. Certificate to both clerks and to court reporters."],["Transcript filed by court reporter",40,"Service of the combined certificate","Iowa R. App. P. 6.803","20 days for guilty pleas and sentencing; 30 days in chapter 232 cases."],["Appellant’s brief",50,"Clerk’s notice of briefing deadline","Iowa R. App. P. 6.901(1)(a)","Halved in expedited cases (Rule 6.902)."],["Appellee’s brief",30,"Service of the appellant’s brief","Iowa R. App. P. 6.901(1)(b)","Or a written statement waiving the brief."],["Appellant’s reply brief",21,"Service of the appellee’s brief","Iowa R. App. P. 6.901(1)(c)","With a cross-appeal the appellant must reply or file a waiver; the cross-appellant then has 14 days to reply."],["Resistance to a motion",14,"Service of the motion","Iowa R. App. P. 6.1002(2)","Reply within 3 days after the resistance."],["Cure of default after clerk’s notice",15,"Issuance of the default notice","Iowa R. App. P. 6.1202(1)","$150 penalty to the lawyer for each default notice."],["Application for interlocutory appeal",30,"Entry of the ruling (or of the ruling on a timely 1.904(2) motion)","Iowa R. App. P. 6.104(1)(c)","15 days in chapter 232 cases."],["Petition for writ of certiorari",30,"Entry of the challenged decision","Iowa R. App. P. 6.107(1)(b)","Runs from the ruling on a timely 1.904(2) or 1.1007 motion."],["Application for discretionary review",30,"Entry of the ruling, order, or judgment","Iowa R. App. P. 6.106(1)(b)",""],["Petition for rehearing in the Court of Appeals",7,"Filing of the Court of Appeals decision","Iowa R. App. P. 6.1204(2)","Does not toll the 20-day further-review period."],["Application for further review",20,"Filing of the Court of Appeals decision","Iowa R. App. P. 6.1103(1)(a)(2)","10 days in chapter 232 cases. Not extendable by the supreme court."],["Resistance to application for further review",10,"Service of the application","Iowa R. App. P. 6.1103(2)(b)","No replies. Not permitted in chapter 232 cases unless requested."],["Petition for rehearing in the supreme court",14,"Filing of the supreme court opinion","Iowa R. App. P. 6.1205(1)","Unless the court shortens or enlarges the time."],["Appeal from denial of anti-SLAPP motion",30,"Entry of the order","Iowa Code § 652.9","Moving party only; proceedings are stayed during the appeal (§ 652.4(3))."],["Motion for new trial or JNOV (trial court)",15,"Filing of the verdict, report, or decision","Iowa R. Civ. P. 1.1007","Court may add up to 30 days for good cause, not ex parte."]],"courts":[],"links":[["Official site","https://www.iowacourts.gov/"]]},"rulesName":"Iowa Rules of Civil Procedure","rulesSrc":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf","rulesAsof":"as checked November 10, 2026","rulesNote":"Each entry is our summary of the rule, not the rule itself. Tap Read the official text before you rely on it.","rules":[{"n":"1.101","t":"Applicability; statutes affected","s":"The chapter governs practice and procedure in all Iowa courts, except where the rules themselves provide otherwise or unaffected statutes prescribe a different procedure for particular courts or cases.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.201","t":"Real party in interest","s":"Every action must be prosecuted by the real party in interest, though executors, administrators, conservators, guardians, express trustees, contracting parties for another’s benefit, and statutorily authorized parties may sue in their own names. No dismissal for lack of the real party until a reasonable time after objection has been allowed for ratification, joinder, or substitution, which then relates back.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.202","t":"Public bond","s":"When a bond or other instrument given to the state, a county, school, or other municipal corporation, or an officer is meant to secure the public or particular individuals, anyone it was meant to protect who was injured by its breach may sue on it in that person’s own name, unless otherwise provided.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.203","t":"Partnerships","s":"Partnerships may sue and be sued as such, or, where law permits, any or all partners may be sued with or without the firm. A judgment against the partnership reaches partnership property and the property of partners served or appearing; a new action lies against partners not served or appearing, and the court may order absent partners brought in.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.204","t":"Foreign corporations","s":"Foreign corporations may sue and be sued in their corporate name unless a statute prohibits it.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.205","t":"Assignees; exception","s":"Outside the Uniform Commercial Code, an assignee of a claim takes it subject to any defense, counterclaim, or claim existing against the assignor in favor of the pleading party, whether or not matured, if matured when pleaded.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.206","t":"Injury or death of a minor","s":"A parent or parents may sue for the expenses and actual loss of services, companionship, and society resulting from injury to or death of a minor child.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.207","t":"Actions by and against state","s":"The state may sue the same way an individual can, and no security may be required of it.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.208","t":"Married persons","s":"A married person may sue or be sued without joining the spouse. If both are sued, each may defend, and if one fails to defend the other may defend for both.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.209","t":"Desertion of family","s":"When a spouse deserts the family, the other spouse may prosecute or defend any action either could have, with the same powers and rights.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.210","t":"Minors; incompetents","s":"An action for a minor or an adjudged incompetent is brought by the conservator, or if none the guardian; otherwise a minor sues by next friend and an incompetent by a court-appointed conservator or guardian. The court may dismiss the action or substitute the representative if that is in the person’s best interest.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.211","t":"Defense by incompetent, prisoner, etc.","s":"No judgment without a defense may be entered against a minor, a person confined in a penitentiary, reformatory, or state mental hospital, an adjudged incompetent, or a party whose physician certifies the party appears mentally incapable of defending. The defense is by guardian ad litem, though the ward’s conservator (or guardian) or the attorney for a competent party may defend unless the fiduciary brought the case or the court supersedes the fiduciary. A trap for out-of-state lawyers: an incarcerated defendant cannot simply be defaulted.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.212","t":"Guardian ad litem","s":"If a served party appears subject to rule 1.211, the court may appoint or substitute a guardian ad litem. The ward (if competent), a minor over 14, the conservator or guardian, or if none any friend or party may apply.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.221","t":"Substitution at death; limitation","s":"Substitution of legal representatives or successors of a deceased party, where a statute permits it, must be ordered within two years after the death. If the claim survives entirely to existing parties, the case continues without substitution. Unlike Fed. R. Civ. P. 25(a)’s 90 days after a statement of death, the Iowa clock is a flat two years from death, and the order (not just the motion) must come within it.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.222","t":"Transfer of interest","s":"Transfer of an interest in a pending action does not abate it but may justify bringing in new parties.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.223","t":"Incapacity pending action","s":"If a party is adjudged incompetent, confined in a state mental hospital, or certified by a physician as apparently mentally incapable during the case, the conservator or guardian must be joined, or if none the court must appoint a guardian ad litem.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.224","t":"Nonabatement in case of guardianship","s":"A pending action does not abate when a conservatorship or guardianship ends; the successor fiduciary, former ward, or the ward’s personal representative is substituted or joined, and if no one applies the court may appoint a personal representative for a deceased party.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.225","t":"Majority of minor","s":"A minor party who reaches majority continues as a party in that person’s own right.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.226","t":"Officers; representatives","s":"When a public official or other representative party leaves that capacity during the suit, the court may order the successor brought in and substituted.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.227","t":"Notice to substituted party","s":"The substitution order fixes the time to appear and the notice to be given; substitution of a deceased party’s legal representative requires notice served like an original notice.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.231","t":"Actions joined","s":"A single plaintiff may join as many legal or equitable, independent or alternative causes of action as the plaintiff has against a single defendant.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.232","t":"Multiple plaintiffs","s":"Any number of persons may join as plaintiffs when they claim relief arising from the same transaction, occurrence, or series and a common question of law or fact is involved, and they may join any related causes of action sharing a common question.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.233","t":"Permissive joinder of defendants","s":"Defendants may be joined when relief is asserted against them jointly, severally, or alternatively arising from the same transaction, occurrence, or series and a common question of law or fact is involved.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.234","t":"Necessary parties; joinder","s":"Persons with a joint interest must be joined on the same side (a reluctant co-plaintiff may be made a defendant), except in class actions and as Iowa Code sections 613.1 and 613.2 allow. A party is indispensable if its interest is not severable and its absence prevents any judgment, or if its interest would be inequitably affected; the court must order indispensable parties brought in and must add other needed parties who can be served, but if jurisdiction over them requires consent the case may proceed without binding them.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.235","t":"Parties partly interested","s":"A party need not be interested in all the relief demanded, and judgment may be given for or against parties according to their respective rights and liabilities.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.236","t":"Remedy for misjoinder","s":"Misjoinder of parties is not a ground for dismissal; parties may be dropped, realigned, or severed at any stage on just terms (the comment notes it tracks Fed. R. Civ. P. 21). The only remedy for misjoinder of causes of action is a motion, on which the court dockets causes separately or strikes them, always keeping at least one in the original case, and the pleader may first withdraw the challenged causes.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.237","t":"Dependent remedies joined","s":"A claim formerly allowed only after another action concluded may be joined with that action, but an action against an indemnitor or insurer may not be joined with one against the insured or indemnified party unless a statute allows it.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.241","t":"Compulsory counterclaims","s":"A pleading must contain a counterclaim for every matured claim against an opposing party arising from the same transaction or occurrence and not the subject of a pending action, unless adjudication would require indispensable parties beyond the court’s jurisdiction. A final judgment on the merits bars an unpleaded compulsory counterclaim.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.242","t":"Permissive counterclaims","s":"Unless a rule or statute prohibits it, a party may counterclaim on any claim held when the action was commenced and matured when pleaded. Unlike Fed. R. Civ. P. 13(b), the claim must have been held at commencement.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.243","t":"Joinder of counterclaims","s":"A counterclaimant may join multiple claims to the same extent a plaintiff may under rules 1.231 and 1.232.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.244","t":"Counterclaim not limited","s":"A counterclaim may diminish or defeat the opposing claim and may seek relief greater than or different from that sought by the opponent.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.245","t":"Cross-claim against coparty","s":"A pleading may assert a cross-claim against a coparty arising from the transaction or occurrence of the original action or a counterclaim, or relating to property at issue, including a claim that the coparty is liable to the cross-claimant for all or part of the claim against it.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.246","t":"Third-party practice","s":"A defending party may file a cross-petition and serve an original notice on a nonparty who is or may be liable for all or part of the plaintiff’s claim, without leave if filed within 10 days after filing the original answer and otherwise by motion on notice. Unlike Fed. R. Civ. P. 14(a)(1), the no-leave window runs from the filing of the answer and the third-party pleading is called a cross-petition; the rest (defenses, claims between plaintiff and third-party defendant, motions to strike or sever, fourth-party claims, and plaintiff’s impleader on a counterclaim) tracks the federal rule.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.251","t":"Right of interpleader","s":"A person exposed to multiple liability or vexatious litigation from several claims to the same thing may bring an equitable interpleader action against all claimants, whose claims need not share a common origin or be identical, and may dispute liability in whole or in part.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.252","t":"By defendants","s":"A defendant exposed to similar liability may obtain interpleader by counterclaim or cross-petition; claimants not before the court may be brought in, and on their default after service the court may bar their claims.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.253","t":"Deposit; discharge","s":"If the party initiating interpleader admits liability for, or nonownership of, the property or amount, the court may order it deposited or secured by bond and, after hearing, discharge the depositor before deciding the competing claims.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.254","t":"Substitution of claimant","s":"When a defendant seeks interpleader involving a third person, that person may appear and be substituted, and the original defendant may be discharged on complying with rule 1.253.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.255","t":"Injunction","s":"After the petition and returns of original notices are filed in interpleader, the court may enjoin all parties before it from starting or prosecuting other suits over the subject until further order.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.256","t":"Costs","s":"Costs may be taxed against the unsuccessful claimant in favor of the successful claimant and the party who initiated the interpleader.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.257","t":"Sheriff or officer; creditor","s":"When an officer is sued over personal property taken under a writ, the writ may be filed with the officer’s affidavit, and the plaintiff must then join the attaching or execution creditor (or the creditor may join on application); any judgment against both must be satisfied first from the creditor’s property.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.261","t":"Commencement of a class action","s":"One or more class members may sue or be sued as representatives if the class is so numerous or so constituted that joinder is impracticable and there is a common question of law or fact. Iowa follows the Uniform Class Actions Rules model rather than Fed. R. Civ. P. 23.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.262","t":"Certification of class action","s":"Unless the court defers it, the court must hold a hearing as soon as practicable after commencement and certify or refuse to certify the class. Certification requires that rule 1.261 is met, a class action is permitted for fair and efficient adjudication, and the representatives will fairly and adequately protect the class; the court may certify by claim, issue, or form of relief and create subclasses.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.263","t":"Criteria considered","s":"In deciding whether a class action is fair and efficient the court weighs 13 listed factors (a through m), including common interest, risk of inconsistent adjudications, predominance, superiority, management and conflict-of-laws difficulties, and whether individual claims are too small to pursue alone. Adequacy requires findings that class counsel is adequate, the representatives have no conflict, and they have or can obtain adequate financial resources considering rule 1.276.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.264","t":"Order on certification","s":"The certification order must describe the class, the relief sought, any claim or issue limitation, and any subclasses, and both a grant and a refusal must state reasons and findings on the rule 1.263(1) factors. An order certifying or refusing to certify is appealable as of right (unlike the discretionary Fed. R. Civ. P. 23(f) petition), and refusal does not end the action but bars class treatment.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.265","t":"Amendment of certification order","s":"The court may amend the certification order any time before judgment on the merits (add subclasses, eliminate members, limit claims or issues, change relief), with notice as directed if notice has already gone out, and must state reasons. An amending order is appealable; an order denying a nonrepresentative defendant-class member’s motion to amend is appealable only if the court certifies it for immediate appeal.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.266","t":"Notice of action","s":"After certification the court must, after a hearing, order notice to the class containing eight listed items, including the right to opt out by a set date and that the judgment binds all who do not. Any member whose potential recovery or liability is estimated to exceed $100 must get personal or mailed notice if identifiable with reasonable diligence; others get notice reasonably calculated to inform them. The plaintiff advances notice costs unless a counterclaim is asserted, in which case the court allocates them.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.267","t":"Exclusion","s":"A plaintiff-class member may opt out unless the member is a representative, the certification order contains a finding under rule 1.263(1)(a), (b), or (c), or a rule 1.270 counterclaim is pending against the member or the member’s class or subclass; a timely election excludes the member from the judgment. Members of a defendant class may not opt out.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.268","t":"Conduct of action","s":"The court may issue orders controlling the course of proceedings, requiring notice to members of steps in the case, the proposed judgment, or chances to participate, imposing conditions on representatives or intervenors, inviting the attorney general to address adequacy, and ensuring adequate representation and competent class counsel. A nonrepresentative member may appear through separate counsel.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.269","t":"Discovery by or against class members","s":"Discovery against a class member who is not a representative party or has not appeared requires a court order, considering timing, subject, duplication, and burden. Representatives and appearing members are subject to ordinary party discovery.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.270","t":"Counterclaims","s":"A defendant may counterclaim against a plaintiff class on any claim the court certifies as a class action, and with leave against a member or subclass; any counterclaim against a plaintiff class must be asserted before class notice is given. The court may stay distribution to a member the losing party is diligently suing elsewhere, a defendant class may counterclaim, a counterclaiming member is treated as a plaintiff-class member for opt-out purposes, and a disallowed or unpleaded counterclaim is not barred in a later action.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.271","t":"Dismissal or compromise","s":"Unless certification was refused, a class action may not be voluntarily dismissed, involuntarily dismissed without a merits ruling, or compromised without court approval after a hearing. Notice of the hearing must go to all members of a certified class (and may be ordered before certification) and must disclose payments, effects, agreements, alternatives considered, and other reasons; the court may approve, deny, or protect the class, and the party seeking dismissal pays notice costs unless agreed or ordered otherwise.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.272","t":"Effect of judgment on class","s":"In a certified class action where notice was given under rule 1.266 or 1.271, the judgment on the certified claims or issues binds every member who did not file an exclusion election, and the judgment must name or describe the members bound.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.273","t":"Costs","s":"Only representatives and individually appearing members are liable for costs assessed against a plaintiff class; the court apportions costs assessed against a defendant class, and advanced notice expenses are taxable as costs for the prevailing party.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.274","t":"Relief afforded","s":"The court may award any relief consistent with the certification order, in a lump sum or installments, but statutory minimum damages may not be recovered in a class action. For money judgments the rule sets a distribution procedure, with identification costs paid from the fund and undistributed amounts going, after hearing and weighing six listed factors, to states as unclaimed property, to the defendant (with possible conditions), or to the Iowa Supreme Court Lawyer Trust Account Commission, with written notice to affected state attorneys general first.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.275","t":"Attorney’s fees","s":"Class counsel fees are subject to court control. Only representatives and individually appearing members are liable for fees owed by a plaintiff class; the court may award fees from a divisible recovery, and for declaratory or equitable relief may shift fees to the adversary if permitted by law in similar cases or if an important public interest was vindicated, weighing six listed factors including the Iowa Rules of Professional Conduct.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.276","t":"Arrangements for attorney’s fees and expenses","s":"Before the certification hearing, or when directed, the representatives and their counsel must file a statement of any amounts paid or promised for services or expenses and their sources, and copies or summaries of fee agreements and fee-sharing agreements with outsiders, supplemented promptly. If costs cannot otherwise be met, the court may authorize and control contributions from class members and advances by attorneys or others, reimbursable from any recovery.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.277","t":"Statute of limitations","s":"Filing a class action tolls limitations for all class members. The statute resumes running against a member on that member’s exclusion election, an order removing the member from the class, an order refusing certification (except for representatives), or dismissal without a merits adjudication.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.278","t":"Virtual representation","s":"When all living members of a class that may grow by later births are parties to an action affecting specific property, those later born are deemed parties and bound by the decree.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.279","t":"Shareholder’s actions","s":"Shareholders of an incorporated or unincorporated association suing to enforce its rights must support the petition by affidavit and allege their efforts to get the directors, trustees, or other shareholders to act, or a sufficient reason for not making the effort.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.281","t":"Expedited civil actions","s":"Plaintiffs whose only relief is money and whose claims by or against any one party total $75,000 or less (counting penalties, prefiling interest, and attorney fees, but not post-filing prejudgment interest, postjudgment interest, or costs) may elect expedited treatment by filing a supreme-court-approved certification signed by all plaintiffs and counsel before the rule 1.507(1) discovery-conference deadline; it does not apply to small claims or domestic relations, and judgments are capped at $75,000 (the jury is not told of the cap) unless the parties proceed by joint motion in an otherwise ineligible case, with termination on changed circumstances or a good-faith compulsory counterclaim exceeding the limits. Absent agreement or good cause, discovery closes 60 days before trial, and each side gets 10 interrogatories, 10 production requests, 10 requests for admission (genuineness requests excepted), one deposition of each party, two nonparty depositions, and one retained expert; only one summary-judgment motion per party, on six listed grounds, filed at least 90 days before trial. Trial must occur within one year of filing on a firm date, to the court unless a rule 1.902 demand is made, with a six-person jury from a panel of 12 (three strikes per side) and a five-juror verdict after at least three hours of deliberation, a six-hour-per-side limit (ordinarily submitted within two business days), records admissible without a custodian on 90 days’ notice (objections within 30 days), health-care-provider statements served at least 150 days before trial (objections within 30 days), no compelled ADR, and no preclusive effect for nonparties.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.301","t":"Commencement of actions; tolling; cover sheet","s":"A civil action is commenced for all purposes, including statutes of limitations, by filing a petition. Every civil petition except small claims, probate, and mental-health commitment must be accompanied by a civil cover sheet, which has no legal effect.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.302","t":"Original notice; form, issuance and service","s":"The summons is called the “original notice”; it must state the court, parties, plaintiff’s counsel contact information, the petition filing date, the response time, the warning that default judgment may be rendered, and the ADA compliance notice, must be signed by the clerk under seal, and is served with a copy of the petition (except publication, where the notice alone, with a general statement of the claim, is published) by any nonparty who is not a party’s attorney. If service is not made within 90 days after filing the petition, the court on motion or its own initiative after notice must dismiss without prejudice as to that defendant or direct an alternate time or manner of service, and must extend the time for good cause.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.303","t":"Time for motion or answer to petition","s":"A defendant must serve a motion or answer within 20 days after service of the original notice and petition, and file it within a reasonable time afterward, unless a specific Iowa statute sets another time. A party served under a court order responds by the date fixed; a party served by publication responds by the date in the published notice, at least 20 days after the last publication; and a party served by mail under rule 1.306 responds by the date in the mailed notice, at least 60 days after mailing. There is no Iowa counterpart to the 60-day waiver-of-service period of Fed. R. Civ. P. 4(d).","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.304","t":"Response of garnishee","s":"The officer serving a writ of attachment or execution garnishes the persons the plaintiff directs by a notice served like an original notice, forbidding payment to the defendant and requiring the garnishee to hold the defendant’s property. Unless statute requires answers to be taken otherwise, the notice cites the garnishee to appear at least 10 days after service and answer interrogatories, on pain of liability for the plaintiff’s judgment.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.305","t":"Personal service","s":"Lists 14 methods of personal service: on an adult by signed, dated acknowledgment, personal delivery, or delivery at the dwelling to a resident at least 18 years old (with rules for rooming houses, hotels, and apartment buildings, and service on a spouse elsewhere if probable cause shows the spouse lives there); on minors and incompetents through conservators, guardians, parents, spouses, or custodians aged 18 or more, with a guardian ad litem when the fiduciary is the plaintiff; on institutionalized persons through the official in charge; on entities through officers, general or managing agents, or registered agents; on local branch offices for business there; on cities (mayor or clerk), counties (auditor or board chair), school districts (president or secretary), the state (per statute), and boards (presiding officer, clerk, or secretary). If none works, service may be made as the court orders consistent with due process.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.306","t":"Alternate method of service","s":"Iowa’s long-arm rule: any corporation, individual, personal representative, partnership, or association with the necessary minimum contacts is subject to Iowa jurisdiction to the full extent of the U.S. Constitution. Service may be made under rule 1.305 in or out of state or, if that cannot be done, in any due-process-compliant manner the court orders, without limiting service methods permitted by statute.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.307","t":"Member of general assembly","s":"No legislator may be required to move or answer in any civil action in an Iowa court while the general assembly is in session.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.308","t":"Returns of service","s":"Sheriffs and deputies (in their own or a contiguous county) and other peace officers, bailiffs, or marshals (in their own territory) may make unsworn returns; all other returns require the server’s affidavit, and private-server fees up to the sheriff’s statutory rate are taxable as costs. The return must state time, manner, and place and the person served (with facts showing compliance for substitute service); proof must be made promptly and within the defendant’s response time, though failure does not invalidate service; mailing is proved by affidavit filed with copies.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.309","t":"Amendment of process or proof of service","s":"The court may allow process or proof of service to be amended at any time on just terms unless it clearly appears that material prejudice to the served party’s substantial rights would result.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.310","t":"Service by publication; what cases","s":"After an affidavit that personal service cannot be had in Iowa, the original notice may be published only in the 12 listed kinds of cases, including actions to recover or partition property, foreclose liens, compel specific performance of a real estate contract, contest or construe a will against a nonresident or unknown party, reach a nonresident’s Iowa property by provisional remedy, affect property in Iowa, reach absconding or concealed residents, dissolve a marriage or quiet title against nonresidents or unknown parties, sue an entity when no one can be personally served, or vacate a judgment under rules 1.1012 and 1.1013.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.311","t":"Known defendants","s":"When a known party is served by publication, a copy of the notice must also be sent by ordinary mail to the last-known address at least 20 days before the response date, unless an affidavit states that no address is known after diligent inquiry. The mailing affidavit (or no-address affidavit) must be filed before judgment, and the court must find that the address used was the last known or that none was known.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.312","t":"Unknown defendants, respondents, or other parties","s":"An original notice to unknown parties is directed to the unknown claimants of the described property and otherwise complies with rule 1.302.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.313","t":"How published","s":"After the petition is filed, the original notice is published once a week for three consecutive weeks in a newspaper of general circulation published in the county of filing, chosen by the plaintiff.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.314","t":"Proof of publication","s":"Before default is taken, proof of publication sworn by the publisher or a newspaper employee must be filed.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.315","t":"Actual service","s":"Personal service under rule 1.305, in or out of Iowa, makes publication unnecessary.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.401","t":"Allowable pleadings","s":"The pleadings are a petition and answer, a reply to a counterclaim so designated, an answer to a cross-claim, a cross-petition when a new party is brought in under rule 1.246, and an answer to a cross-petition. Iowa calls the complaint a “petition” and a third-party complaint a “cross-petition.”","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.402","t":"General rules of pleading","s":"Pleadings are judged by the rules and construed to secure a just, speedy, and inexpensive decision on the merits; averments must be simple, concise, and direct, alternative and inconsistent claims or defenses are allowed, and motions are not pleadings. The court may order prolix or confused pleadings recast. A party may amend once as of course before a responsive pleading is served, or within 20 days after service if no responsive pleading is required and the case is not on the trial calendar; otherwise only with leave (freely given) or the adversary’s written consent. Amendments must be on a separate paper; relation back, including for a changed party who had notice within the limitations period and knew of the mistake, tracks Fed. R. Civ. P. 15(c) (though Iowa’s notice window is the limitations period itself, without the federal 90-day service period).","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.403","t":"Claims for relief","s":"A claim needs a short and plain statement showing entitlement to relief and a demand for the type of relief sought. Except in small claims and purely liquidated-damages cases, the pleading must not state a specific dollar amount, only whether jurisdictional amount requirements are met; amounts are obtained in discovery. The petition must state whether it is at law or in equity.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.404","t":"Appearances","s":"An appearing attorney must identify the attorney or attorneys in charge, not merely the firm, and is then entitled to service under rule 1.442. An appearance alone does not prevent default or other orders, but any party who has appeared must get notice and an opportunity to respond to a motion for default judgment under rule 1.973(2). Limited-scope appearances under Iowa R. Prof’l Conduct 32:1.2(c) must be noticed in advance or simultaneously and announced at the start of a hearing, and end without leave when the attorney files and serves a notice of completion containing the listed information.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.405","t":"Answer","s":"The answer must state on whose behalf it is filed, specifically admit or deny each allegation or paragraph (lack of information is a permitted denial), plead additional defensive facts, and put any counterclaim in a separate division; answers for wards must address service and deny all prejudicial material allegations. Facts not denied are admitted, except allegations of value or amount of damage, averments needing no response, and new facts pleaded within seven days before trial, which are deemed denied. A copied signature of an adverse party is deemed genuine unless denied by that party’s affidavit (with inspection on application during the time to plead); a nonparty’s signature on a nonnegotiable writing is deemed genuine unless denied by affidavit.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.406","t":"Reply","s":"The court may order a reply to an answer or to an answer to a cross-petition.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.407","t":"Interventions","s":"On timely application, intervention is of right when a statute gives an unconditional right or when the applicant has an interest the action may impair that existing parties do not adequately represent, and is permissive for a conditional statutory right, a common question of law or fact, or a government officer or agency administering a statute or regulation relied on, considering undue delay or prejudice. The movant serves a motion with grounds and an accompanying pleading; the intervenor has no right to delay and pays the costs of intervention unless it prevails. The comment says the rule is substantially similar to Fed. R. Civ. P. 24.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.411","t":"Caption and signature","s":"Every appearance, notice, motion, or pleading must carry a caption naming the court, parties, and instrument and the filer’s signature, personal identification number, address, phone, and, if available, fax and e-mail; first papers must include each named party’s identification number if available, and the petition caption must state law or equity. Social security numbers go on a separately filed confidential information form, not on pleadings.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.412","t":"Paragraphs; separate statements","s":"Averments must be in numbered paragraphs each limited, as far as practicable, to a single set of circumstances, and each claim on a separate transaction and each defense other than denials must be in a separate count or defense when that aids clarity.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.413","t":"Verification abolished; affidavits; certification","s":"Pleadings need not be verified unless a statute requires it. Counsel’s signature certifies reading, reasonable inquiry, factual and legal grounding, and no improper purpose; unsigned papers are stricken unless promptly signed, and a violation requires (“shall”) an appropriate sanction against the signer, the represented party, or both, which may include expenses and fees. Unlike Fed. R. Civ. P. 11, there is no 21-day safe-harbor provision in the text; discovery papers are governed by rule 1.503(6). A plaintiff who unsuccessfully prosecuted three or more actions in the preceding five years may be required, if they were frivolous, to post cost security; motions asserting facts and pleadings seeking interlocutory relief need supporting affidavits; and verification may be replaced by an Iowa Code section 622.1 certification under penalty of perjury.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.414","t":"Supplemental pleadings","s":"With leave on reasonable notice and just terms, or the adversary’s written consent, a party may serve and file a supplemental pleading about later events, even if the original pleading is defective; no response is required unless ordered.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.415","t":"Judicial notice; statutes","s":"Judicially noticed matters, including Iowa statutes, need not be pleaded. A pleading relying on another jurisdiction’s statute must identify it plainly, and the court must then take judicial notice of it.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.416","t":"Negligence; mitigation","s":"In negligence actions by an employee against an employer or a passenger against a common carrier, the plaintiff need not plead or prove freedom from contributory negligence, but the defendant may plead and prove it in mitigation of damages.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.417","t":"Permissible conclusions; denials","s":"Capacity, corporate authority to sue or do business in Iowa, performance of conditions precedent, and judgments of special-jurisdiction tribunals may be pleaded as conclusions. A denial of such an averment must state the facts relied on, not merely contradict it.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.418","t":"Contract","s":"Every pleading referring to a contract must state whether it is written or oral.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.419","t":"Defenses to be specially pleaded","s":"Defenses that a contract or writing is void or voidable or was delivered in escrow, and any matter of justification, excuse, release, discharge, or confession and avoidance, must be specially pleaded.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.420","t":"Account; bill of particulars; denial","s":"A pleading on an account must include a bill of particulars by consecutively numbered items, which defines and limits the proof. A responding pleading must specify the items denied, and unspecified items are deemed admitted.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.421","t":"Defenses; how raised; consolidation; waiver","s":"Every defense must be asserted in the responsive pleading or in an amendment to the answer made within 20 days after the answer is served, or at trial if no response is required. Six matters may be raised by pre-answer motion: subject-matter jurisdiction, personal jurisdiction, insufficient original notice or service, to recast or strike, for a more specific statement, and failure to state a claim; improper venue under rule 1.808 must be raised by pre-answer motion. Motions on grounds (b) through (f) existing at the time must be combined in one motion (only one per pleading unless amended), and omitted grounds are waived except subject-matter jurisdiction and failure to state a claim; sufficiency of a defense is tested by motion to strike before pleading to it, and every motion must specify the claimed insufficiency.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.422","t":"Protected information","s":"Counsel and parties, not the clerk, must omit or redact social security numbers, financial account numbers, personal identification numbers, and other unique identifiers from filings unless material or required by law (using only the last four digits of a social security number and incomplete account numbers when needed), including in initially confidential records that later become public. Filers may also redact 11 listed categories, such as driver’s license numbers, medical information, employment history, personal financial information, trade secrets, home addresses, dates of birth, and names of minor children.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.423","t":"Limited representation pleadings and papers","s":"A pro se party’s paper drafted with a limited-scope attorney’s help must say so before the signature line and list the attorney’s (or the nonprofit program’s) name and contact information, but the attorney does not sign it. The drafting attorney must ensure the paper is well grounded and not improper, may rely on the client’s facts unless there is reason to doubt them, and is not thereby appearing or entitled to service.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.431","t":"Motion practice; generally","s":"A motion is not a pleading but is subject to rule 1.413(1) certification and must be captioned, signed, and state its specific points, with an optional concise brief. Unless otherwise ordered or provided, a written resistance is due within 10 days after service of the motion, and a reply limited to new authority or unanticipated matters is due within seven days after the resistance or before the hearing, whichever is earlier; evidence may be by affidavit, and affiants may be required to appear. The court must rule within 30 days after submission unless it extends the time on the record, may decide without hearing or by telephone (paid for by the movant unless agreed), and temporary-order hearings in dissolution and paternity matters must be held with the parties present by the same means the court uses.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.432","t":"Failure to move; effect of overruling motion","s":"A pleading is not held sufficient merely because no one moved against it, and a party whose motion was overruled does not waive the error by pleading over and may keep challenging the pleading’s sufficiency.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.433","t":"Motion for more specific statement","s":"A party may move for a more specific statement only of matter too indefinite to permit a response, pointing out the defect and the particulars desired.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.434","t":"Motion to strike","s":"Improper or unnecessary matter in a pleading may be stricken on the adverse party’s motion.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.435","t":"Motion days; submission of pretrial motions","s":"Each district’s chief judge sets monthly motion days in each county, when all pretrial motions on file 14 days or more are deemed submitted unless a hearing is set or another time is fixed by rule, statute, or order for good cause; served or appearing parties are charged with notice of motion day. The court may rule earlier to avoid delay and may set other hearing times to expedite, not delay.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.441","t":"Time to move or plead","s":"Motions attacking a pleading must be served before responding, or within 20 days after service if no response is required. The answer is due under rule 1.303; answers to cross-claims and replies to counterclaims are due 20 days after service (or 20 days after an order requiring a reply). After a motion is decided in a way requiring further pleading, the pleading is due 10 days after notice of the ruling; responses to amended pleadings are due within the remaining original time or 10 days after service of the amendment, whichever is longer. The court may shorten times, may extend the time to answer or reply by no more than 30 days for good cause but not ex parte, and may allow a late answer on terms; a notice of removal suspends state jurisdiction until remand, after which all times start anew.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.442","t":"Service and filing of pleadings and other papers","s":"Every filed paper, later pleading, discovery paper, written motion, notice, appearance, demand, and offer of judgment must be served on each party, but not on defaulted parties except for new claims, which require original-notice service under rule 1.305. Service on a represented party is on the attorney (limited-appearance counsel only for the covered proceedings) by delivery, mail (complete on mailing), or fax, or by e-mail only with written consent naming the address (complete on transmission unless the sender learns it failed). Papers are filed before or within a reasonable time after service, and filing deadlines are tolled by timely service if filing follows within a reasonable time; legal briefs and memoranda may not be filed except for summary judgment unless ordered, but are served with a copy delivered to the presiding judge and a certificate of compliance. The clerk mails notice of each order or judgment to all nondefaulted parties and dockets the mailing.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.443","t":"Enlargement; additional time after service","s":"For cause the court may enlarge a period, with or without motion, if asked before it expires, or after expiration on motion for excusable neglect, but it may not extend the times for exceptions, judgment notwithstanding the verdict, or new-trial motions (rules 1.1001, 1.1003, 1.1004) except as rule 1.1007 allows. Three days are added when a paper triggering a deadline is served by mail, e-mail, or fax, but not when the court prescribed the service method and notice period or when the deadline runs from entry or filing of a judgment, order, or decree. Unlike Fed. R. Civ. P. 6(d), e-mail service still adds three days.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.444","t":"Pleading over; election to stand","s":"When a ruling requires or permits further pleading, the clerk mails or delivers notice to counsel, and unless the ruling says otherwise the party must file within 10 days after that mailing or delivery. A party who does not plead within that time is deemed to elect to stand on the record, and the ruling becomes a final adjudication in the trial court without further judgment as to the issues it disposes of; this can silently start appeal time.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.451","t":"Specific rulings required","s":"A motion or other matter with separate grounds or parts must be ruled on separately as to each, not sustained generally.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.452","t":"Order defined","s":"Every written direction of the court not included in the judgment or decree is an order.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.453","t":"When and how entered","s":"A judge may enter judgments, orders, or decrees any time after submission, effective when filed with the clerk (or as rule 1.442(5) allows), and the clerk must promptly send notice or a copy to each appearing party or one of its attorneys, including by e-mail to the address provided.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.455","t":"Preliminary determination","s":"On any party’s application, a motion for judgment on the pleadings under rule 1.954 and the defenses of lack of subject-matter or personal jurisdiction, improper venue, insufficient process, insufficient service, failure to state a claim, and failure to join a party under rule 1.234 must be decided before trial unless the court defers them to trial.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.456","t":"Cross-petition, cross-claim, counterclaim; judgment","s":"Judgment on the original claim is not delayed by a pending cross-petition, cross-claim, or counterclaim if it can be entered without prejudice to the rights at issue in them.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.457","t":"Amending to conform to the evidence","s":"Issues tried by express or implied consent are treated as if pleaded, and amendments to conform may be made at any time, even after judgment, though failing to amend does not affect the result. When evidence is objected to as outside the pleadings, the court must freely allow amendment unless the objector shows prejudice, and may grant a continuance.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.458","t":"Special action; proper remedy awarded","s":"In mandamus, certiorari, appeals to the district court, or actions for specific equitable relief, if the facts pleaded and proved show entitlement to a different remedy, the court must let the petitioner amend to request it on terms, and may award it.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.500","t":"Duty to disclose; required disclosures","s":"Without a request, parties must disclose witnesses with contact information and subjects, copies of documents and ESI they may use (or a good-cause statement and description), a computation of each damages category (not exact noneconomic amounts), and insurance declarations pages (plus the policy and coverage letters if coverage is contested); personal-injury claimants must also give date of birth, Medicare number, all providers seen from five years before the injury to the present, and records waivers, and lost-earnings claimants must give five years of tax returns, five years of employers, and employment-records waivers. Domestic relations parties instead exchange six months of pay information, three years of tax returns, a financial affidavit, and supporting statements (modification and unmarried custody cases only the first two), within 60 days after the petition is filed; 12 categories of proceedings are exempt; other initial disclosures are due at or within 14 days after the rule 1.507 conference, or within 30 days for later-joined parties, and incomplete investigation or an insurer’s possession is no excuse. Unless the scheduling order says otherwise, expert disclosures (with a signed report for retained or regularly testifying employee experts, including publications from the previous 10 years and testimony from the previous four years) are due 90 days before trial and rebuttal disclosures within 30 days after the other side’s; pretrial witness, deposition, and exhibit disclosures are due at least 14 days before trial (stipulations may not go below 7 days), with objections within 7 days (or 4 days if the disclosures were due less than 10 days before trial), and unmade objections other than relevance and Rule 5.403 objections are waived. The rule applies to actions commenced on or after January 1, 2015.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.501","t":"Discovery methods","s":"Beyond rule 1.500 disclosures and subject to rule 1.505 timing, discovery is by depositions on oral or written questions, interrogatories, production or entry, physical and mental examinations, and requests for admission, liberally construed to give access to all relevant facts. Discovery must be in good faith with responses meeting the substance of requests, and every discovery motion must certify, with date and time, that the movant personally spoke or tried to speak with affected parties to resolve the dispute. An oath requirement may be satisfied by an unsworn certification under penalty of perjury under Iowa law.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.502","t":"Discovery materials not filed","s":"Unless the court orders otherwise, depositions, notices, interrogatories, production requests, requests for admission, responses, produced materials, and objections are not filed. A rule 1.517 motion attacking the sufficiency of a response must attach the request and response or may be denied; perpetuation depositions under rules 1.721 to 1.728 are excepted.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.503","t":"Scope of discovery","s":"Discovery extends to any nonprivileged matter relevant to the subject matter of the action, including information reasonably calculated to lead to admissible evidence, with “documents” including ESI, but always subject to the proportionality limits of rule 1.503(8). Unlike current Fed. R. Civ. P. 26(b)(1), Iowa’s text retains the “subject matter” and “reasonably calculated” formulation, while adding the federal proportionality factors as mandatory limits. The rule also covers discovery of insurance agreements (not thereby admissible), work product (substantial need and undue hardship, with opinion work product protected and a person’s own prior statement obtainable on request), a duty to timely supplement witness identities and material matters without any knowing-concealment requirement, express privilege logs and clawback of inadvertently produced privileged material, a Fed. R. Civ. P. 26(g)-style signature certification for disclosures and discovery papers with mandatory sanctions, and reliance by any party on other parties’ discovery.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.504","t":"Protective orders","s":"For good cause, on motion of a party or affected person, the court where the action is pending (or, for depositions, where the deposition is taken) may issue any order justice requires to prevent annoyance, embarrassment, oppression, or undue burden or expense, including the eight listed forms, and must limit discovery under rule 1.503(8). ESI from sources not reasonably accessible need not be produced unless the requester shows good cause after the producing party shows undue burden or cost. A protective-order motion needs a good-faith conference certification with dates and times, and expenses follow rule 1.517(1)(d).","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.505","t":"Timing and sequence of discovery","s":"No party may seek discovery from any source before the rule 1.507 conference, except in proceedings exempt from initial disclosures or as authorized by rule, stipulation, or order; in domestic relations cases discovery waits for the rule 1.500(1)(d) disclosures. The moratorium applies to actions commenced on or after January 1, 2015, and discovery methods may otherwise be used in any sequence without one party’s discovery delaying another’s.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.506","t":"Stipulations regarding discovery procedure","s":"Unless the court orders otherwise, the parties may by written stipulation provide for depositions before any qualified person at any time, place, notice, or manner and may modify other discovery procedures.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.507","t":"Discovery conference of the parties","s":"Except in exempt proceedings, domestic relations cases, or as ordered, the parties must confer as soon as practicable and no later than 21 days after any defendant has answered or appeared (or 21 days after a small-claims transfer is docketed); the plaintiff must notify all parties of the deadline, and a pre-answer motion does not suspend the conference or initial disclosures. The parties must discuss claims, settlement, disclosures, preservation, and a discovery plan, and must submit a written report within seven days after the conference (and before the trial-setting conference) on Iowa Court Rule 23.5 Form 2, covering six listed subjects; any party may then request a rule 1.602 pretrial conference. Unlike Fed. R. Civ. P. 26(f), the clock runs from the first answer or appearance, not from a scheduling conference.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.508","t":"Discovery of experts","s":"A party may depose any expert identified to testify, but only after any required rule 1.500(2)(b) report is provided, and may obtain expert reports, models, and materials, including consultant material that forms a basis of a testifying expert’s opinions; draft reports and most attorney-expert communications are protected as in Fed. R. Civ. P. 26(b)(4). A nontestifying consultant is discoverable only under rule 1.516 or exceptional circumstances, unless the consultant’s work underlies a testifying expert’s opinions. Supplements to an expert’s report and deposition must be disclosed no later than 30 days before trial, trial testimony may not go beyond the fair scope of the disclosures, and the discovering party pays the expert a reasonable fee (capped at the customary rate, including deposition and travel time but not preparation time).","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.509","t":"Interrogatories to parties","s":"Except in small claims, any party may serve interrogatories, which must be provided in electronic word-processing format, and answers and objections are due within 30 days after service, with each interrogatory set out immediately before its answer (failure counts as a failure to answer), answers signed under oath, and specific objections signed by counsel; unstated grounds are waived. A party may not serve more than 30 interrogatories on another party, including discrete subparts, without stipulation or leave on a written motion showing good cause, but each supreme-court-approved pattern interrogatory counts as one. Interrogatories may ask for the specific dollar amount of damages and itemized amounts, contention interrogatories are allowed, and a business-records option is available.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.510","t":"Requests for admission","s":"A party may serve requests to admit facts, opinions of fact, application of law to fact, and genuineness of documents, but no more than 30 per party without agreement or leave on a written motion. Matters are admitted unless a signed answer or objection is served within 30 days after service (a defendant need not respond before 60 days after service of the original notice unless the court shortens it); denials must fairly meet the substance, lack of knowledge requires a statement of reasonable inquiry, and a genuine issue for trial is not a valid objection. The requester may move to test sufficiency, with expenses under rule 1.517(1)(d).","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.511","t":"Effect of admission","s":"A rule 1.510 admission is conclusively established for the pending action unless the court on motion allows withdrawal or amendment, which it may do when that serves presentation of the merits and the requesting party fails to show prejudice, subject to rule 1.604. Admissions are only evidentiary admissions in other proceedings.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.512","t":"Production of documents, electronically stored information, and things; entry upon land for inspection and other purposes","s":"A party may request inspection, copying, testing, or sampling of designated documents, ESI, and tangible things in another party’s possession, custody, or control, or entry on land, described with reasonable particularity and with a reasonable time, place, and manner. A written response is due within 30 days after service, with specific objections stating whether anything is being withheld (unstated grounds are waived), production by the inspection date or a stated reasonable later time, ESI in the requested or a reasonably usable form, and documents produced as kept or organized by request category. The supreme court may approve pattern requests.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.514","t":"Action for production or entry against persons not parties","s":"Rule 1.512 does not preclude an independent action against a nonparty for production of documents and things or permission to enter land.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.515","t":"Physical and mental examination of persons","s":"When the mental or physical condition (including blood group) of a party or a person in a party’s custody or legal control is in controversy, the court may, only on motion for good cause and notice to the person and all parties, order an examination by a health care practitioner (not only a physician), specifying time, place, manner, conditions, scope, and examiner.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.516","t":"Report of health care practitioner","s":"On request of the examined person or the party ordered, the party causing the examination must deliver the examiner’s detailed report and earlier reports on the same condition, and must then receive like reports from the other side; an examiner who will not report may be excluded at trial. Requesting the report or deposing the examiner waives privilege as to every other examiner of the same condition in that or any action on the same controversy, and the rule applies to examinations by agreement unless the agreement says otherwise.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.517","t":"Consequences of failure to make disclosures or discovery","s":"A party may move to compel disclosures, deposition answers, entity designations, interrogatory answers, or production (evasive or incomplete answers count as failures), and an unresisted motion may be granted without hearing once the resistance time expires; every order granting such a motion must warn that noncompliance may bring sanctions, and the clerk serves it on counsel and the party. Expenses and fees shift to the loser unless substantially justified; disobeying an order allows the Fed. R. Civ. P. 37(b)-type sanctions (deemed facts, preclusion, striking, dismissal, default, contempt except for examinations, and mandatory expenses); undisclosed information or witnesses are excluded unless substantially justified or harmless, and failure to admit later proven matters shifts proof costs. Total failure to attend one’s deposition or to respond to interrogatories or production requests is sanctionable and is not excused by objectionability absent a protective-order motion; no discovery motion may be filed without a certification of personal conferral with date and time; routine good-faith loss of ESI is not sanctionable absent exceptional circumstances; and failing to participate in framing the rule 1.507 plan can bring expenses.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.601","t":"Pretrial calendar","s":"The court may provide for a pretrial calendar in any county, covering all actions or limited to jury or nonjury cases.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.602","t":"Pretrial conferences; scheduling; management","s":"The court may order attorneys and unrepresented parties to pretrial conferences to expedite disposition, manage the case, and facilitate settlement. On application or its own motion (except in exempt categories) the court must enter a scheduling order setting deadlines for joinder, expert designation, discovery, amendment, and motions, may add ESI, privilege, complex-case, and conference provisions, and may modify the schedule only for good cause; it may take up 16 listed subjects at conferences, and at least one attorney for each party must have authority to stipulate. A final pretrial conference is held as close to trial as reasonable, attended by trial counsel; failure to obey orders, nonappearance, substantial unpreparedness, or bad faith may bring rule 1.517(2)(b)(2)–(4) sanctions plus mandatory expenses unless justified.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.603","t":"Pretrial conference; record","s":"On request of any interested counsel or the court, the reporter must record all or any designated part of a pretrial conference.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.604","t":"Pretrial orders","s":"After any rule 1.602 conference the court enters an order reciting the action taken, which controls the case unless modified. The order after the final pretrial conference must follow rule 1.1901 Form 6 and may be modified only to prevent manifest injustice.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.701","t":"Depositions upon oral examination","s":"Any party may depose any person without leave, compelling attendance by rule 1.715 subpoena, except that leave is needed (absent stipulation) to depose before the rule 1.505(1) moratorium ends, to depose a person already deposed in the case, or to depose a prisoner; a plaintiff may depose early without leave on a certified notice that the witness is about to leave the state and will be unavailable unless deposed before 10 days after the defendants’ answer date, but the deposition cannot be used against a party who diligently could not obtain counsel. Oral depositions may be taken only in Iowa or within 100 miles of the nearest Iowa point unless the court orders otherwise after hearing, and a party or an entity party’s officer, partner, or managing agent must be deposed in the county where the action is pending unless ordered otherwise. Nonstenographic recording requires a court order unless also taken stenographically; the noticing party may be charged expenses for failing to attend or to subpoena the witness; and any deposition may be taken by telephone on written notice, at the initiating party’s untaxable expense, with the reporter with the witness unless agreed. The text contains no federal-style 10-deposition or one-day, seven-hour limit.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.702","t":"Depositions in small claims","s":"In small claims, no deposition may be taken without leave of court obtained on notice and a showing of just cause, on just terms.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.703","t":"Deposition notice to parties in default","s":"A party who must prove up a judgment against a defaulted party may take depositions after serving notice on the defaulted party’s attorney of record, or on the defaulted party if unrepresented; defaulted parties are not otherwise entitled to deposition notice.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.704","t":"Use of depositions","s":"Admissible parts of a deposition may be used at trial, interlocutory hearings, or motion hearings against any party who appeared, stipulated, or had notice: to impeach; for any purpose if the deponent was an adverse party or an adverse entity’s officer, partner, or managing agent; for any purpose if the witness could not be subpoenaed, is out of state (not procured by the offeror), dead, or unable to testify; for any purpose if the deponent was a specially retained expert or a health care practitioner testifying about a party’s condition; and on application in exceptional circumstances. Unlike Fed. R. Civ. P. 32(a)(4), the depositions of retained experts and treating health care practitioners may be used regardless of availability, and the distance test is simply absence from the state.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.705","t":"Effect of taking or using depositions","s":"If part of a deposition is offered, others may require the relevant remainder and offer other relevant parts. Taking a deposition, or using it only to impeach or as an adverse party’s, does not make the deponent the offeror’s witness, but using it for another purpose does, though the offeror may still contradict it.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.706","t":"Substituted parties; successive actions","s":"Substitution does not prevent using depositions already taken, and depositions from a dismissed action may be used in a later action on the same subject between the same parties or their representatives or successors.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.707","t":"Notice for oral deposition","s":"Reasonable written notice to every party must state the time, place, and the deponent’s name and address or an identifying description, and must include any subpoena duces tecum designation; a notice to a party deponent may include a rule 1.512 production request. No subpoena is needed for a party or an entity party’s officer, partner, or managing agent, and an organization may be noticed or subpoenaed with matters described with reasonable particularity, in which case it must designate persons to testify on information known or reasonably available to it (the counterpart of Fed. R. Civ. P. 30(b)(6)).","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.708","t":"Conduct of oral deposition","s":"Examination proceeds as at trial before an officer who swears the witness and records the testimony (transcribed on request); objections are noted and evidence taken subject to them, must be concise, nonargumentative, and nonsuggestive, and instructions not to answer are allowed only to preserve privilege, enforce a court limitation, or present a motion to terminate or limit. Parties may instead submit sealed written questions. The court may sanction anyone who impedes, delays, or frustrates a fair examination, and on a showing of bad faith or oppression may terminate or limit the deposition, which is suspended on demand while the motion is made, with expenses under rule 1.517(1)(d).","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.709","t":"Reading and signing depositions","s":"A deposition reported by an official court reporter or Iowa certified shorthand reporter need not be read or signed by the deponent. In other cases the transcript is submitted to the witness for review, changes are entered with reasons, and the witness signs unless waived or unable; if not signed within 30 days after submission, the officer signs and notes why, and the deposition may be used unless suppressed under rule 1.717(6). Unlike Fed. R. Civ. P. 30(e), review is not something the witness must request, and the default exemption for Iowa certified reporters means most Iowa depositions are never signed.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.710","t":"Depositions on written interrogatories","s":"A party may take a deposition on written questions after serving all nondefaulted parties with the questions and a notice naming the officer and deponents. Cross-interrogatories are due within 10 days after the notice, redirect within five days after that, and recross within three days after that, subject to court adjustment for good cause; within the cross-interrogatory period any party may instead elect to cross-examine orally, and the deposing party must then within five days notice the date, time, and place.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.711","t":"Answers to interrogatories","s":"The party taking a written deposition promptly sends the notice and all questions to the officer, who takes the answers and completes the deposition under rules 1.708 and 1.709, though not necessarily stenographically.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.712","t":"Certification and return; copies","s":"The officer certifies that the witness was sworn and the record is true; documents produced are marked and annexed on request, though copies may be substituted after verification or originals returned after marking and inspection, and any party may move to have originals filed. The officer must furnish copies to any party or the deponent on payment of reasonable charges.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.713","t":"Before whom taken","s":"The officer may not be a party, financially interested, a party’s attorney or employee, or related within the fourth degree to a party, an attorney, or their employees, and may not enter reporting agreements that take control of the original transcript before certification, give one party special terms not offered to all, or give any party an exclusive advantage. Domestic depositions may be taken before anyone authorized to administer oaths under U.S., Iowa, other-state, or local law; foreign depositions before U.S. diplomatic or consular officers or under rule 1.714; and military witnesses before a commanding commissioned officer or a JAG officer.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.714","t":"Letters rogatory","s":"A commission or letters rogatory for a foreign deposition issue only when convenient or necessary, on application and notice and on just terms, specifying the officer by name or title, and may be addressed to the appropriate judicial authority of the country.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.715","t":"Deposition subpoena","s":"On a party’s application or proof of service of a deposition notice, the clerk where the action is pending issues subpoenas for the persons named, and subpoenas may also issue by statute or rule 1.1701. No Iowa resident may be subpoenaed to attend more than 50 miles from where the person resides, is employed, or transacts business in person.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.716","t":"Costs of taking deposition","s":"The deposing party pays the costs of a deposition and cannot use it in evidence until they are paid; the judgment taxes against the loser only the portion necessarily incurred for testimony offered and admitted at trial.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.717","t":"Irregularities and objections","s":"Objections to a deposition notice are waived unless promptly served in writing; objections to the officer’s qualifications unless made before the deposition begins or when discoverable; objections to the form of written interrogatories unless served within the time for the next round (three days for the last); and curable errors in conduct, oath, or form of question or answer unless seasonably made at the deposition. Other evidentiary objections may be made at trial, and objections to transcription, signing, certification, or handling must be made by a reasonably prompt motion to suppress, granted only for substantial, material defects.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.721","t":"Common law preserved","s":"Rules 1.722 through 1.728 do not limit the court’s common-law power to entertain actions to perpetuate testimony.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.722","t":"Application before action","s":"An application to perpetuate testimony before suit is filed in the court where the action might be brought, captioned in the applicant’s name, supported by affidavit, and must show five things: the applicant expects to be a party to an Iowa action that cannot yet be brought, its subject and the applicant’s interest, the facts to be shown and reasons for perpetuating, the expected adverse parties, and each deponent’s name, address, and expected testimony.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.723","t":"Notice of application","s":"The applicant must serve each expected adverse party with notice of the hearing and a copy of the application, in the manner of an original notice other than publication, at least 20 days before the hearing; if diligent service fails, the court may order publication or other service, or on extraordinary circumstances a hearing on less than 20 days’ notice.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.724","t":"Guardian ad litem","s":"Before the hearing the court must appoint an attorney as guardian ad litem for anyone under legal disability or not personally served, to cross-examine at any ordered deposition; without that appointment the deposition is inadmissible against that person later.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.725","t":"Order allowing application","s":"If the application is not for discovery, its allowance may prevent future delay or failure of justice, and the applicant cannot bring the action, the court must order the testimony perpetuated, designating deponents, subject matter, time, place, officer, and whether oral or written.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.726","t":"Taking and filing testimony","s":"Perpetuation depositions are taken as ordered and otherwise under rules 1.708 to 1.713 and 1.717, and unless the court enlarges the time must be filed within 30 days after the date fixed for taking them or they cannot later be received in evidence.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.727","t":"Limitations on use","s":"In a later action involving an expected adverse party named and served under rule 1.723, or that party’s privies or successors, any party may use the perpetuation deposition or a certified copy if the deponent is dead, mentally ill, or unavailable.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.728","t":"Perpetuating testimony pending appeal","s":"During the time to appeal or while an appeal is pending, the trial court may on motion (stating deponents, expected testimony, and reasons) allow testimony to be perpetuated for possible further proceedings if needed to avoid a failure or delay of justice and not for discovery; the depositions are then treated as if taken while the case was pending.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.801","t":"Grounds for change","s":"The place of trial may be changed on motion if the county is a party and an adverse party has demanded a jury on a jury-triable issue; if the trial judge is directly interested or related within the fourth degree to a party; for prejudice of the judge or county inhabitants or an adverse party’s undue influence, shown by affidavits of the movant and three disinterested, unrelated persons, with counter-affidavits allowed; by written agreement; or when a nonresident defendant sued on a written contract performable in the county files a sworn answer alleging fraud in the inception, in which case the case goes to the defendant’s home county and the defendant must post a cost bond within 10 days after the transfer order and bears the plaintiff’s resulting expenses if it loses.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.802","t":"Limitations","s":"No change of venue is allowed in an appeal from small claims; for county prejudice in a bench trial (except prejudice of the judge); before the issues are made up (unless the objection is to the judge); after a continuance (except for new or unknown causes); or after one change, for causes then existing and reasonably ascertainable. No party may have more than two changes.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.803","t":"Subsequent change","s":"After a trial following a venue change ends in a hung jury or a new trial is granted, the court may allow another change under rule 1.801(1)–(4), subject to rule 1.802.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.804","t":"Of whole case","s":"A change may be granted on one coparty’s motion, and the whole case is then transferred unless separate trials are ordered under rule 1.914.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.805","t":"Where tried","s":"Except for fraud-in-contract transfers, the case goes to a convenient county in the same judicial district, or another district if the ground applies to all its counties; if the ground concerns only the judge, the court may instead obtain another judge, or the supreme court may designate one.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.806","t":"Costs","s":"Except for changes by agreement or for fraud in contract, the order designates the costs of the change, which the movant must pay before it is perfected; failure to pay within 10 days after the order waives the change of venue.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.807","t":"Transferring cause","s":"Once a change is ordered and costs paid, the clerk sends a transcript and original papers to the new court, keeping an authenticated copy, and the case is docketed there without fee.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.808","t":"Action brought in wrong county","s":"An action filed in the wrong county may proceed there unless a defendant moves before answering for transfer to the proper county; the court must then transfer at the plaintiff’s cost, which may include the defendant’s expenses and attorney fees for attending in the wrong county. If those costs are not paid within 20 days after the transfer order, the action must be dismissed. Unlike federal practice, wrong venue is not a ground for dismissal, only for transfer.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.901","t":"Trials and issues","s":"A trial is a judicial examination of issues of law or fact. An issue of fact arises when a material factual allegation is denied by pleading or by operation of law; all other issues are issues of law and must be tried first.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.902","t":"Demand for jury trial","s":"Jury trial is waived unless a written demand is made no later than 10 days after the last pleading directed to the issue; a demand may be in a pleading (noted in the caption) or filed separately and served, and once filed may not be withdrawn without consent of all nondefaulted parties. An unlimited demand covers all jury-triable issues; after a limited demand, another party may demand other issues within 10 days or a shorter ordered time. The court may still order a jury on motion for good cause, not ex parte. Unlike Fed. R. Civ. P. 38(b)’s 14 days after service of the last pleading, Iowa’s window is 10 days.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.903","t":"Trial of issues; reporting","s":"Issues are tried to the court unless a jury is demanded, and demanded issues go to a jury unless there is no right to one or all appearing parties waive it in writing or orally in open court. Unless waived, all trial proceedings must be reported, including the 10 listed categories (court comments, jury selection, openings, testimony, exhibits, motions, closings, verdict); the reporter files a memorandum on rule 1.1901 Form 12 serving as the Iowa Code section 624.10 certification, and transcript rates follow Iowa Ct. R. 22.28.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.904","t":"Findings by court","s":"In a nonjury trial the court must make written findings of fact and separate conclusions of law, no request is needed, and sufficiency of the evidence may be challenged on appeal without objection below. A motion to reconsider, enlarge, or amend findings and conclusions must be joined with or filed within the time for a new-trial motion (under rule 1.1007, 15 days after the verdict, report, or decision is filed), and a rule 1.904(2) motion directed at any other order, ruling, judgment, or decree is timely if filed within 15 days after it is filed; resistances and replies follow rule 1.431. Successive rule 1.904(2) motions are barred unless aimed only at a modification, and per the comment any timely rule 1.904(2) motion extends the appeal deadline.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.905","t":"Exceptions unnecessary","s":"Formal exceptions are unnecessary once a matter has been raised by objection, motion, or otherwise and the court has ruled.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.906","t":"Civil trial-setting conference","s":"Except in domestic relations, the clerk must send notice of a trial-setting conference (Iowa Ct. R. 23.5 Form 1) to nondefaulted parties no later than 21 days after any defendant answers or appears, setting the conference between 35 and 50 days after that answer or appearance. The parties must obtain a timely conference whether or not they receive notice, and lack of notice is no excuse from dismissal under rule 1.944; trial is set using Form 2, and a continued trial must be reset to a date certain with prior deadlines still applying.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.907","t":"Trial assignments","s":"The court (or a delegated court administrator) may assign a case for trial at the end of a scheduling, pretrial, or trial-setting conference, by agreement of all parties, or on its own motion after consulting counsel; dissolution and small claims trials may be set without consultation, subject to rescheduling for conflicts. At least twice each month the clerk presents to a judge each small claims appeal taken more than 20 days earlier, which is decided on the record without oral argument unless a party filed a written request specifying the issues within 20 days after the appeal was taken; no new evidence is received except as a statute allows.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.908","t":"Duty to notify court","s":"Attorneys or self-represented parties must notify the court immediately when a case assigned for trial settles. An attorney actually engaged in trial elsewhere when the case is reached must tell the court, which may hold the case in abeyance, and must notify the court and stand ready as soon as the other engagement ends.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.909","t":"Fee for late settlement of jury trial","s":"If a party waives a jury or gives notice of settlement later than two full working days before a civil jury trial is scheduled or reached (whichever is later), or the case settles during trial, a $1,000 fee is assessed as court costs. The court may not waive it or grant a continuance to avoid it, and the money goes to the state general fund.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.910","t":"Motions for continuance","s":"A continuance motion must be filed without delay once the grounds are known and may be amended only to fix clerical errors. No case assigned for trial may be continued ex parte, and every continuance motion in a case set for trial must be signed by counsel and approved in writing by the represented client unless the court waives that approval.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.911","t":"Causes for continuance","s":"A continuance may be granted for any cause not due to the movant’s fault or negligence if substantial justice will be better served, and must be granted if all parties agree and the court approves. A motion based on absent evidence needs an affidavit giving the witness’s name and residence (or diligence to find them), diligent efforts and grounds to expect the testimony by a specified date, and the particular facts expected that no other witness can fully prove; the opponent can defeat a sufficient motion by admitting the witness would so testify.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.912","t":"Objections; ruling; costs","s":"The opponent may file specific written objections to a continuance motion at once or within a reasonable time allowed. Where defenses are distinct, the case may be continued as to fewer than all defendants, and every continuance is at the movant’s cost unless ordered otherwise.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.913","t":"Consolidation","s":"Unless a party shows prejudice, the court may consolidate actions involving common questions of law or fact or order a joint trial of any issues, with orders to avoid unnecessary cost or delay.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.914","t":"Separate trials","s":"For convenience or to avoid prejudice, the court may order separate trials of any claim, counterclaim, cross-claim, cross-petition, or issue, and may sever any claim against a party.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.915","t":"Impaneling jury","s":"Sixteen jurors are drawn (by ballot or computer), sworn, and examined by the parties and the court; the court decides all challenges for cause. A challenge to the panel must be in writing before any juror is sworn and can rest only on a material departure from statutory requirements, and individual challenges must come before the jury is sworn, on 12 listed grounds including felony conviction without restored rights, relationship to the adverse party within the ninth degree, being a client of a lawyer’s firm in the case, and an unqualified opinion on the merits. Each side strikes four, alternating after challenges (the court may allow more with multiple separately represented parties), and the eight jurors remaining are sworn; unlike Fed. R. Civ. P. 48, the civil jury is a fixed eight.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.916","t":"Saturday a religious day","s":"Before final submission, no juror whose faith requires observing Saturday as a religious day can be compelled to attend that day.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.917","t":"Juror incapacity; minimum number of jurors","s":"If a juror becomes unable to act or is disqualified before the jury retires, the rest continue; if more than two jurors drop out before verdict (leaving fewer than six), the court must declare a mistrial.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.918","t":"Returning ballots to box","s":"When the jury is sworn, ballots of absent or excused jurors return to the box at once, and ballots of sworn jurors are set aside and returned when that jury is discharged.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.919","t":"Procedure after jury sworn","s":"The party with the burden on the whole action may make an opening statement, followed by the other party; the burdened party then presents evidence, followed by the adverse party, and then only rebuttal unless the court allows otherwise. Only one counsel per side may examine a given witness unless the court permits.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.920","t":"Further testimony for mistake","s":"Before final submission, the court may allow further testimony to correct an evident oversight or mistake, on just terms.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.921","t":"Adjournments","s":"After trial begins, the court may adjourn it for such time and on such conditions as justice requires.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.922","t":"View","s":"The court may order an officer to take the jury as a body to view property or a place where a material fact occurred, and no one else may speak to them about the case during the view.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.923","t":"Arguments","s":"The parties may submit or argue the case; the party with the burden opens and closes, must disclose all relied-on points in opening, and if the close raises new material points the adversary may reply, closing argument. A party waiving opening is limited in closing to reply. The court may limit argument time to itself but not jury argument time.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.924","t":"Instructions","s":"The court must give written, consecutively numbered instructions on all material issues, read without comment (oral only if the parties agree). Parties may file written requests at the close of evidence or earlier if the court sets a time; the court gives a preliminary draft on controversial issues and then final instructions before jury argument, and all objections must be made in writing or dictated into the record outside the jury’s presence before argument, specifying the matter and grounds, or are lost on appeal; objections to later revisions must be raised in the new-trial motion. The court may read final instructions earlier in trial after counsel review, and must repeat them at the end.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.925","t":"Additional instructions","s":"During deliberations the court may give further written instructions in counsel’s presence or after notice; they are filed as part of the record, and objections are made in the motion for new trial.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.926","t":"Materials available to jurors","s":"Jurors may take notes on court-provided materials and must be told the notes are not evidence and will be destroyed after deliberations. Jurors take their notes and all exhibits into deliberations unless ordered otherwise, but depositions go only if all evidence is in writing and none was stricken.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.927","t":"Separation and deliberation of jury","s":"A sworn jury may separate only by court order with admonitions not to discuss the case or form opinions. After submission the jury is kept together by an officer until verdict or discharge, except as the court permits overnight, weekends, holidays, or emergencies, and the officer may communicate only to ask whether they have agreed.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.928","t":"Discharge; retrial","s":"The court may discharge a jury for accident or calamity, by consent, when a continuance follows an amendment, or when it is clear the jurors cannot agree, and the case is retried immediately or later as directed.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.929","t":"Court open for verdict","s":"While the jury is out, the court may adjourn other business but stays open for every purpose connected with the case until verdict or discharge.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.930","t":"Food and lodging","s":"The court may order food and lodging at state expense for a jury kept together.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.931","t":"Rendering verdict and answering interrogatories","s":"The parties may stipulate before return that a stated majority may decide; otherwise verdicts and answers must be unanimous, except that all jurors but one may decide after at least six hours of deliberation following submission. Unlike Fed. R. Civ. P. 48(b), which requires unanimity absent stipulation, Iowa allows a 7-of-8 verdict after six hours. The finding is read in court, any party may require a poll, and the jury is sent back if the required number does not agree; a sealed verdict by consent is equivalent to rendition and cannot be polled.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.932","t":"Form and entry of verdicts","s":"Verdicts and answers must be in writing, signed by the jury’s foreperson if unanimous or by all concurring jurors if not, are sufficient if they express the jury’s intent, and are filed and entered after being put in form by the court if needed.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.933","t":"Special verdicts","s":"The court may require a special verdict on each fact issue by written questions or proposed findings, with instructions enabling a finding on each. A party who does not demand submission of an omitted fact issue before the jury retires waives jury trial on it, and the court may find it or it is deemed found consistent with the judgment; comparative-fault interrogatories under Iowa Code chapter 668 are treated as special verdicts.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.934","t":"Interrogatories","s":"With a general verdict the court may, and on any party’s request must, submit written fact interrogatories, shown to adverse counsel before argument. If answers are consistent with each other but not the verdict, the court may enter judgment on the answers, order a new trial, or send the jury back; if the answers are inconsistent with each other and the verdict, it must send the jury back or order a new trial. Unlike Fed. R. Civ. P. 49(b), submission is mandatory on request.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.935","t":"Reference to master","s":"A master includes a referee, auditor, or examiner, and may be appointed only on a showing of exceptional conditions and only for issues not tried to a jury; the clerk gives the master the appointment order.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.936","t":"Compensation","s":"The court fixes the master’s pay and orders it paid by parties or from a fund as just, enforceable by execution on the master’s demand; the master may not hold reports as security.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.937","t":"Powers","s":"The order may limit the master’s powers, issues, and deadlines or confine the master to taking evidence; otherwise the master regulates proceedings, administers oaths, compels witnesses and evidence, rules on admissibility, makes a record of excluded evidence on request, and may appoint a reporter whose fees the requesting party advances.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.938","t":"Speedy hearing","s":"The master must notify the parties of a first meeting within 20 days after appointment or as ordered, may proceed ex parte or adjourn if a party fails to appear, must act diligently, and may be ordered by the court to expedite or report.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.939","t":"Witnesses","s":"Parties may subpoena witnesses before a master as for trial, and a witness who fails to appear or testify without good cause faces the same consequences as in court.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.940","t":"Accounts","s":"The master may prescribe the form for accounts, receive a testifying CPA’s statement, and, on objection or insufficiency, require a different form or proof by testimony or written interrogatories of the accounting parties.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.941","t":"Filing report","s":"The master files the original exhibits, any transcript or else a summary, and a report with findings and conclusions if ordered, and may first circulate a draft to counsel.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.942","t":"Disposition","s":"The clerk mails notice of the report’s filing to counsel, and any party may file written objections within 10 days after mailing unless the court enlarges the time. The court must accept the master’s fact findings unless clearly erroneous and may adopt, reject, modify, or recommit the report; if the parties stipulated that findings are final, only questions of law remain.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.943","t":"Voluntary dismissal","s":"A party may dismiss its own petition, counterclaim, cross-claim, cross-petition, or petition of intervention without court order at any time up until 10 days before trial is scheduled to begin; after that only with court consent on terms, and with the consent of a counterclaimant unless the counterclaim can stand independently. A dismissal is without prejudice unless stated otherwise, but a second dismissal of an action including or based on the same cause against the same defendant, after a prior dismissal in any state or federal court, operates as an adjudication on the merits unless the court orders otherwise in the interests of justice. Unlike Fed. R. Civ. P. 41(a)(1), the unilateral right lasts until 10 days before trial rather than ending at the answer or summary-judgment motion.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.944","t":"Uniform rule for dismissal for want of prosecution","s":"Iowa’s policy is that civil cases be tried within one year after filing. Every case whose petition was filed more than one year before July 15 of a year must be tried before January 1 of the next year; the clerk sends notice before August 15, and unless satisfactory reasons are shown by application, ruled on after notice and not ex parte, the case is dismissed without prejudice at the plaintiff’s cost. Exceptions (with findings of record) cover cases on appeal or under submission, post-judgment proceedings, Servicemembers Civil Relief Act stays, cases with claims being paid under stipulation or order, and cases awaiting a master, and no dismissal occurs on a timely showing that process has not been served despite due diligence; continuances require a court order (not stipulation alone), and reinstatement is discretionary, and mandatory for oversight, mistake, or other reasonable cause, on application filed within six months after dismissal.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.945","t":"Involuntary dismissal","s":"A party may move to dismiss a claim or for another order when the claimant fails to comply with the rules or a court order. After a party rests, the adversary may move to dismiss for failure to show a right to relief without waiving the right to offer evidence.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.946","t":"Effect of dismissal","s":"Dismissals not governed by rule 1.943 and not for lack of jurisdiction or improper venue operate as adjudications on the merits unless they say otherwise.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.947","t":"Costs of previously dismissed action","s":"When a plaintiff sues again on a claim previously dismissed against the same defendant in any state or federal court, the court may stay the new suit until the costs of the prior action are paid.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.951","t":"Judgment defined","s":"Every final adjudication of any of the parties’ rights in an action is a judgment.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.952","t":"Partial judgment","s":"A party who succeeds only in part may have judgment expressly for the successful part and against it on the rest.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.953","t":"As to some parties only","s":"In a multiparty action the court may render judgment for or against some parties only, whenever the prevailing party would have been entitled to it had that party been alone or a several judgment is proper, leaving the action to continue as to the others.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.954","t":"Judgment on the pleadings","s":"After the pleadings, a party may move for judgment on the pleadings.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.955","t":"On verdict","s":"The clerk must immediately enter judgment on a filed verdict unless it is special or the court has reserved the case for further argument or consideration.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.956","t":"Principal and surety; order of liability","s":"A judgment against a principal and surety must recite the order of their liability, and “surety” includes anyone whose liability is secondary to another’s.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.957","t":"On claim and counterclaim","s":"A claim and counterclaim may not be set off against each other unless both parties agree or a statute requires it. If either side’s obligation is likely uncollectible, the court may order both to pay into court for distribution, deciding separate set-off issues independently and treating payments as if made between the parties.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.959","t":"Entry","s":"All judgments and orders must be entered on the court record and clearly specify the relief granted or order made.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.960","t":"Taxation of costs","s":"If the court fails to assess costs on disposition, the clerk notifies the judicial officer, and if no assessment is made within 10 days after that notice, the clerk enters judgment for costs against the party who initiated the action.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.961","t":"Notes surrendered","s":"Unless the court specially orders otherwise, the clerk may not enter a judgment on a note or other written evidence of debt until the original is filed with the clerk for cancellation.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.962","t":"Affidavit of identity","s":"The clerk may not enter a personal judgment until the creditor or its agent or attorney files an affidavit of the debtor’s full name, occupation, and residence (with street residence and business addresses if the debtor lives in an incorporated place of more than 5,000 people), though a judgment entered without it is not invalid.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.971","t":"Default defined","s":"A party is in default when it fails to serve, and within a reasonable time file, a motion or answer as required by rule 1.303 or 1.304; withdraws a pleading without leave to replead; fails to appear for trial; fails to comply with any court order; or does any act permitting default under a rule or statute.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.972","t":"Procedure for entry of default","s":"The clerk enters defaults for failure to plead or withdrawal of a pleading against parties not under legal disability or imprisoned, without court order; all other defaults are entered by the court. The written application must certify that written notice of intent to seek default (substantially as rule 1.1901 Form 10) was mailed by ordinary mail to the defaulting party’s last known address, and to any attorney known to represent it even if not appearing, after the default occurred and at least 10 days before the application was filed, counted from mailing, with a copy of the notice attached. The 10-day notice does not apply in small claims, forcible entry and detainer, juvenile cases, or against parties served by publication; this is a common trap because no federal counterpart requires pre-default notice to a nonappearing party.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.973","t":"Judgment on default","s":"For a sum certain or computable sum, the clerk on request and affidavit that the amount is due enters judgment for that amount and costs. In all cases the court, on the prevailing party’s motion, orders the judgment to which it is entitled, after notice and opportunity to respond for any party who has appeared, and may (and on demand of a nondefaulted party must) take evidence, refer to a master, or submit to a jury if one was properly demanded under rule 1.902.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.974","t":"Notice of default in certain cases","s":"When a default judgment (other than in rem) is taken against a party served by substituted service on another person under rule 1.305(1), the clerk must immediately mail written notice to that party’s last known address or the service address and record the mailing, though failure to do so does not invalidate the judgment.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.975","t":"On published service","s":"No personal judgment may be entered against a person served only by publication, or publication and mailing under rule 1.311, unless that person has appeared.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.976","t":"Relief in other cases","s":"A default judgment may award any relief consistent with and within the issues of the petition, but if the defaulting party never appeared it cannot exceed what was demanded.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.977","t":"Setting aside default","s":"On motion, for good cause, on terms, and not ex parte, the court may set aside a default or default judgment for mistake, inadvertence, surprise, excusable neglect, or unavoidable casualty. The motion must be filed promptly after the grounds are discovered and no more than 60 days after entry of the judgment, and filing does not affect the judgment’s finality or operation. Unlike Fed. R. Civ. P. 55(c) and 60(b), there is a hard 60-day outer limit.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.981","t":"Summary judgment: on what claims","s":"A claimant may move for summary judgment on all or part of a claim at any time after the appearance day or after the adverse party moves, and a defending party at any time, with or without affidavits. Unless the court orders otherwise, the motion must be filed at least 60 days before the trial date, a resistance (with a statement of disputed facts, a memorandum, and any affidavits) is due within 15 days after service of the motion, and the hearing or submission may be no sooner than 20 days after the motion is filed; the motion must attach a separate concise statement of undisputed material facts with record citations and a memorandum. The standard is no genuine issue of material fact and entitlement to judgment as a matter of law, liability-only interlocutory judgment is allowed, the court specifies uncontroverted facts when it does not decide the whole case, affidavits must be on personal knowledge with sworn or certified attachments, the opponent must set out specific facts, the court may continue the motion for discovery, and bad-faith affidavits bring expenses, fees, and possible contempt.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.982","t":"On motion in other cases","s":"Judgment may be obtained by motion by sureties against principals or cosureties for amounts paid, by clients against attorneys, by execution plaintiffs against sheriffs or officers for money or property collected, and where statutes authorize. Contribution based on comparative fault may be obtained by motion only on fact findings already made in that action and only by or against persons who were parties when the findings were made; the motion may be filed after final judgment and during an appeal, and the judgment may be a declaratory judgment conditioned on future payment.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.983","t":"Summary judgment: procedure","s":"A rule 1.982 motion in a pending action follows rule 1.981 procedure; otherwise it must be served with notice of the hearing at least 10 days before the hearing, and the court hears it without further pleadings and enters judgment.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.1001","t":"Bill of exceptions","s":"A bill of exceptions is needed only to show material parts of the record not reflected in the court files, entries, or certified reporter’s notes. No more than five supporting affidavits may be filed with it, and up to five controverting affidavits within seven days after, extendable for good cause; the judge must sign a bill that fairly presents the facts, and if the judge refuses, counsel’s certification plus the written attestation of at least two bystanders makes it part of the record. A successor or other judge of the court may sign if the trial judge or master cannot.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.1002","t":"New trial defined","s":"A new trial is a reexamination in the same court of any fact issue or part of one after a verdict, master’s report, or court decision.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.1003","t":"Judgment notwithstanding verdict","s":"On motion a party may get judgment despite an adverse verdict or a jury’s failure to return one if the adverse pleadings omit a material fact necessary to a complete claim or defense (specified in the motion), or if the movant moved for and was entitled to a directed verdict at the close of all the evidence, in which case the court may grant a new trial or enter judgment as if it had directed the verdict. Unlike Fed. R. Civ. P. 50, the predicate directed-verdict motion must come at the close of all the evidence.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.1004","t":"New trial","s":"On motion, an aggrieved party may have a verdict, decision, or report vacated in whole or part and a new trial granted for nine listed causes that materially affected substantial rights: irregularity or abuse of discretion preventing a fair trial, jury or prevailing-party misconduct, unguardable accident or surprise, damages influenced by passion or prejudice, error in the amount in contract or property actions, insufficient evidence or a result contrary to law, newly discovered material evidence, errors of law or mistakes of fact by the court, and any rule 1.1003 ground.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.1005","t":"Motion; affidavits","s":"Motions for judgment notwithstanding the verdict and new trial must be in writing, and those based on misconduct, accident or surprise, or newly discovered evidence may be supported and opposed by affidavits and heard under rule 1.431(6).","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.1006","t":"Stay","s":"When a timely motion for judgment notwithstanding the verdict or new trial, or a rule 1.1012 petition, is filed, the court may in its discretion stay further proceedings and enforcement on terms pending disposition.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.1007","t":"Time for motions and exceptions","s":"Motions for judgment notwithstanding the verdict and for new trial, and bills of exception, must be filed within 15 days after the verdict, report, or decision is filed with the clerk (or the hung jury is discharged), unless the court for good cause and not ex parte grants additional time of no more than 30 days. Unlike Fed. R. Civ. P. 50(b) and 59’s 28 days after entry of judgment, the Iowa clock is 15 days from filing of the verdict or decision; resistances and replies follow rule 1.431.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.1008","t":"Conditional rulings on grant of motion","s":"Filing one post-trial motion does not waive others. A party whose verdict is set aside by judgment notwithstanding the verdict may move for a new trial within 15 days after that judgment is entered, and a court granting judgment notwithstanding the verdict must also conditionally rule on any new-trial motion and state grounds, with the conditional ruling governing what happens if the judgment is reversed on appeal.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.1009","t":"Issues tried by consent; amendment","s":"In ruling on post-trial motions, the court treats issues actually tried by express or implied consent as pleaded, and parties may amend to conform, though failure to amend does not affect the result.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.1010","t":"Conditional new trial","s":"The court may let a party avoid a new trial by accepting terms, such as remittitur or additur. Consent to a reduced, modified, or increased judgment must be filed with the district court clerk no later than seven days before the appeal deadline under Iowa R. App. P. 6.101 if imposed by the district court, or within 30 days after procedendo is filed if imposed by an appellate court, or the new trial is automatically ordered. If there is an appeal, a district-court-imposed condition or judgment is void and the original verdict judgment is reinstated.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.1011","t":"Retrial after published notice","s":"Except in dissolution and annulment cases, a defendant who did not appear and was served only by publication (or publication and mailing) may apply for retrial within six months after entry of judgment and, on posting security for costs, is entitled to defend as if no judgment existed. After retrial the court may confirm, modify, or vacate the judgment and order restitution.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.1012","t":"Grounds for vacating or modifying judgment","s":"On a timely petition under rule 1.1013, the court may correct, vacate, or modify a final judgment or order, or grant a new trial, for six grounds: clerk’s mistake, neglect, or omission; irregularity or fraud in obtaining it; erroneous proceedings against a minor or person of unsound mind not shown in the record; a party’s death before entry without substitution; unavoidable casualty or misfortune preventing prosecution or defense; or newly discovered material evidence not discoverable in time for a new-trial motion.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.1013","t":"Procedure for vacating or modifying judgment","s":"The petition requires the filing fee under Iowa Code section 602.8105(1)(a) (or section 631.6(1)(a) in small claims), must be filed and served in the original action within one year after entry of the judgment or order, must state the grounds (and for a new trial, why they could not be found in time for rules 1.977 or 1.1004), must allege a meritorious claim or defense if the original pleadings did not, and must be supported by affidavit. It is served like an original notice, set for trial no sooner than 20 days after service, deemed denied without answer, and tried like an ordinary bench case with no new claims; the court may decide the grounds first, and if the judgment is affirmed after a rule 1.1006 stay the petitioner owes the costs and, in the court’s discretion, damages of up to 10 percent of the judgment. Unlike Fed. R. Civ. P. 60(b), relief requires a separate fee-paid petition with original-notice service, and the outer limit is one year for all grounds.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.1014","t":"Disposition of exhibits","s":"One year after final determination, the clerk may destroy filed exhibits after written notice to counsel that they will be destroyed unless receipted for within 60 days; the clerk may destroy all trial exhibits without notice two years after final determination.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.1015","t":"Titles and liens protected","s":"A good-faith purchaser’s title to property sold under the original judgment is not affected by proceedings under rules 1.1011 to 1.1013, and liens and security obtained under a judgment that is merely modified carry over to the modified judgment.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.1016","t":"Judgment discharged on motion","s":"When matters discharging a judgment arise after its entry, the defendant or any interested person may move to have it discharged in whole or in part.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.1017","t":"Fraudulent assignment; motion","s":"On motion, the court may inquire into the assignment of a judgment or its entry for a party’s use and cancel or strike it in whole or part if inequitable, fraudulent, or in bad faith.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.1018","t":"Execution; duty of officer","s":"An officer receiving an execution must act diligently, levying on debtor property likely to bring as nearly the exact amount as practicable (with successive levies if needed), collecting or selling things in action (suing in the officer’s own name if necessary), and selling or garnishing enough to satisfy the writ, paying proceeds less costs to the clerk.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.1019","t":"Endorsement","s":"The officer must endorse on the execution the day and hour received, each levy, sale, or other act with its date, and the date and amount of each payment, at the time of the act, though a late endorsement does not impair a levy or sale.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.1020","t":"Levy on personalty","s":"A levy on personal property under attachment or general execution creates no lien until the officer either takes possession and appends a signed, dated, detailed description to the writ, or, on the creditor’s written request, views the property, appends a detailed inventory and a statement of case, parties, amount, location, possessor, and debtor’s address, and files a certified transcript with the secretary of state, where it is indexed like a financing statement and gives constructive notice; a termination statement is filed on satisfaction or discharge, with filing fees taxed as costs.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.1101","t":"Declaratory judgments permitted","s":"Courts of record may declare rights, status, and other legal relations whether or not further relief could be claimed, in affirmative or negative form with the effect of a final decree; another available remedy does not bar declaratory relief, and the specific instances in rules 1.1102 to 1.1104 do not limit this power.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.1102","t":"Construing contracts, etc.","s":"Anyone interested in an oral or written contract or will, or whose rights are affected by a statute, ordinance, rule, regulation, contract, or franchise, may obtain a determination of its construction or validity and a declaration of rights under it.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.1103","t":"Before or after breach","s":"A contract may be construed before or after a breach.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.1104","t":"Fiduciaries, beneficiaries and others","s":"Persons interested as or through fiduciaries, creditors, devisees, legatees, heirs, next of kin, or trust beneficiaries in a trust or an estate of a decedent, insolvent, minor, or ward may obtain a declaration to ascertain a class of creditors or heirs, to direct fiduciaries to act or refrain, or to resolve administration questions including construction of wills and other writings.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.1105","t":"Discretionary","s":"The court may refuse a declaratory judgment that would not end the uncertainty or controversy.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.1106","t":"Supplemental relief","s":"Further relief based on a declaratory judgment may be sought by petition in the original case, and if sufficient the court orders adverse parties whose rights were adjudicated to show cause, on reasonable notice, why it should not be granted.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.1107","t":"Review","s":"Declaratory orders, judgments, and decrees are reviewable like other judgments.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.1108","t":"Jury trial","s":"The declaratory judgment rules neither abridge nor extend the right to jury trial.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.1109","t":"“Person”","s":"For declaratory judgments, “person” includes any individual or entity capable of suing or being sued under Iowa law.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.1301","t":"Quo warranto: for what causes","s":"A civil action in the nature of quo warranto, tried in equity, may be brought in the state’s name against anyone unlawfully holding a public office, franchise, or Iowa corporate office; a public officer whose act works a forfeiture of office; anyone acting as an Iowa corporation without authority; a corporation exceeding its powers or forfeiting its privileges; or a holder of a state patent, permit, certificate, or license obtained by fraud or mistake, expired, violated, or forfeited, to annul it.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.1302","t":"By whom brought","s":"The county attorney may bring quo warranto and must when directed by the governor, general assembly, or supreme or district court, with the attorney general acting if the county attorney may be a defendant. If the county attorney refuses a citizen’s demand, the attorney general may sue, or the citizen may obtain leave and, after posting a court-fixed cost bond, prosecute the action.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.1303","t":"No joinder or counterclaim","s":"No other claim may be joined in a quo warranto action, and no counterclaim is allowed.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.1304","t":"Petition","s":"The quo warranto petition must state its grounds and name others claiming the office, franchise, or right, who may be made parties.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.1305","t":"Judgment","s":"The judgment must determine all parties’ rights, including who is entitled to any disputed office, remove or forfeit unlawfully held offices, franchises, or privileges (including a corporation’s forfeited privileges), and prohibit further exercise of powers improperly used where forfeiture is not warranted.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.1306","t":"Costs","s":"Judgment against a defendant or intervenor includes costs, assessed against the individuals acting for a pretended corporation; if the action fails the court may tax costs to a private relator, otherwise they are paid as in criminal cases.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.1307","t":"Corporation dissolved","s":"If the judgment dissolves a corporation, the court must enter appropriate dissolution orders under the governing statutes.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.1401","t":"Certiorari petition","s":"A party may bring certiorari when a statute authorizes it or when an inferior tribunal, board, or officer exercising judicial functions, or a judicial magistrate, exceeded its jurisdiction or otherwise acted illegally.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.1402","t":"Certiorari: procedure","s":"The petition is captioned with the petitioner as plaintiff against the tribunal, board, or officer, proceeds by ordinary proceedings, and must be filed within 30 days after the challenged illegal act. The reviewing court may extend the time only if the delay resulted from the tribunal’s failure to notify the petitioner, on a motion filed within 90 days after the decision, supported only by portions of the record and affidavits.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.1403","t":"Other remedies","s":"Certiorari is not denied because another plain, speedy, or adequate remedy exists, but unless a statute provides otherwise relief is strictly limited to questions of jurisdiction and legality.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.1404","t":"The writ","s":"A district judge may order a writ to an inferior tribunal, board, officer, or magistrate, issued by the clerk under seal, commanding certification of the pertinent records and proceedings and the facts at a specified time and place.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.1405","t":"Stay, bond","s":"The court may stay the original proceedings even if no stay is requested, and may condition a requested stay on a bond with security for costs as it prescribes.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.1406","t":"Notice of issuing writ","s":"The court may issue the writ without notice when the petition is filed or set a hearing on reasonable notice, but must set a noticed hearing first if the petition precedes a final order below or a stay is sought; the hearing is confined to the petition’s sufficiency, what is to be certified, and any bond.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.1407","t":"Service of writ","s":"A sheriff or deputy serves the writ by delivering the original (unless service is accepted), on the magistrate or that court’s clerk, or a board’s secretary, clerk, or member, and returns a copy with the return of service to the issuing clerk.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.1408","t":"Return to writ, by whom","s":"The return is made and signed by the magistrate whose decision is challenged if practicable (otherwise that court’s clerk), by the officer to whom the writ issued, or by a board’s presiding officer, clerk, or secretary.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.1409","t":"Defective return","s":"If the return is defective, the court may order a further return on its own or a party’s motion and enforce the writ or order by attachment or contempt.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.1410","t":"Certiorari: hearing","s":"After a full return, the court sets a hearing and may receive transcripts, recordings, and explanatory evidence, considered only on the legality of the proceedings or sufficiency of the evidence below unless a statute provides otherwise.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.1411","t":"Certiorari: judgment","s":"Unless a statute provides otherwise, the judgment only annuls or sustains the writ in whole or part to the extent the proceedings were illegal or beyond jurisdiction, prescribes how the parties may proceed, and may not substitute a different decree or order.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.1412","t":"Certiorari: appeal","s":"Appeals from district court certiorari rulings follow the ordinary civil appellate rules, but review is discretionary when the district court ruling was itself a discretionary review of another tribunal.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.1501","t":"Independent or auxiliary remedy","s":"An injunction may be sought as an independent equitable action or as auxiliary relief in any action, with damages or other relief in the same case, and may be granted in the judgment or earlier by order as a temporary injunction.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.1502","t":"Temporary; when allowed","s":"A temporary injunction may issue when the petition, supported by affidavit, shows entitlement to relief restraining an act that would greatly or irreparably injure the plaintiff; when during the case a party is doing or threatening an act violating the other’s rights in the subject that would make the judgment ineffectual; or when a statute specially authorizes it.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.1503","t":"Endorsing refusal","s":"A court or supreme court justice refusing a temporary injunction must endorse the refusal on the petition.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.1504","t":"Statement re prior presentation","s":"A petition for a temporary injunction must state, or counsel must certify on it, whether the same relief has previously been presented to and refused by any court or justice, and if so by whom and when.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.1505","t":"Place for filing","s":"A temporary-injunction request must be filed in the county where the action is or will be pending.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.1506","t":"By whom granted","s":"A temporary injunction may be granted by a judge of the district where the action is or will be pending, or by the supreme court or a justice of it.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.1507","t":"Injunctions: notice","s":"The court may require notice and hearing before a temporary injunction, and an applicant seeking one without notice must have counsel certify in writing the efforts made to notify the adversary or its attorney or why notice should not be required. Notice and hearing are mandatory for temporary injunctions or stays of agency action under Iowa Code section 17A.19(5) and to halt a corporation’s ordinary business, a state agency’s action, railway or municipal operations, construction of a building or other work, a county board of supervisors, or a nuisance.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.1508","t":"Injunctions: bond","s":"Before a temporary injunction issues, the order must require a bond in a stated penalty of 125 percent of the probable liability, with clerk-approved sureties, conditioned to pay damages caused by the injunction; in dissolution, separate maintenance, annulment, and domestic abuse cases the court may waive the bond or set any reasonable amount. Unlike Fed. R. Civ. P. 65(c), the amount is fixed by formula at 125 percent.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.1509","t":"Hearing to dissolve temporary injunction","s":"A party enjoined without notice may at any time move the court where the action is pending to dissolve, vacate, or modify the injunction, and a hearing must be held within 10 days after the motion is filed.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.1510","t":"Enjoining proceedings or judgment; venue; bond","s":"An action to enjoin proceedings in a civil case or on a judgment or final order must be brought in the county and court where they are pending or were obtained (or, for a supreme court judgment, the court appealed from), and any bond must also be conditioned to pay or comply with the judgment or any judgment recovered on the enjoined claim.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.1511","t":"Violation as contempt","s":"Violating any temporary or permanent injunction is contempt and punishable accordingly.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.1601","t":"Applicability of rules","s":"The civil procedure rules apply to judicial review of agency action under Iowa Code chapter 17A except where inconsistent with that Act or this division.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.1602","t":"Time for motion or answer","s":"In judicial review under Iowa Code section 17A.19(2), the respondent must serve a motion or answer on the petitioner and all others required to be served within 20 days after personal service or mailing of the petition, and file it within a reasonable time thereafter.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.1603","t":"Contested case proceedings; intervention; schedule","s":"In judicial review of a contested case under Iowa Code section 17A.19, an intervenor may side with either party or claim adversely to both, and the court must set a schedule on any party’s request and may on its own.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.1701","t":"Subpoena","s":"A subpoena must state the court, title, and docket number, command attendance, production of documents, ESI, or things, or inspection at a stated time and place, state the recording method for depositions, and set out the text of rules 1.1701(4) and (5); the clerk issues signed blank subpoenas on oral or written request, and Iowa-authorized attorneys may issue them. Any nonparty at least 18 years old may serve within Iowa (elsewhere only if a statute allows and the court authorizes), tendering one day’s attendance and travel fees if demanded, and notice to each party must precede service of a pretrial documents or inspection subpoena; proof is by a statement certified under Iowa Code section 622.1. Mirroring Fed. R. Civ. P. 45, the issuer must avoid undue burden on pain of sanctions, written objections are due before the earlier of the compliance date or 14 days after service, the court must quash for unreasonable time, travel by a nonparty more than 50 miles from residence, work, or business (though trial attendance anywhere in Iowa may be ordered), privilege, or undue burden, ESI, privilege-log, and clawback rules apply, any party may attend, the issuer must share copies at cost, and unexcused noncompliance is contempt.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.1702","t":"Uniform interstate depositions and discovery","s":"A party to an action in another state may obtain an Iowa subpoena by submitting the foreign subpoena to the clerk in the county where discovery will occur, who issues a signed blank subpoena (opening a file for a $50 fee), or by having an Iowa-authorized attorney issue it, which is not an appearance. The subpoena must comply with rule 1.1701 using the Iowa county court and the foreign caption and docket number, list all counsel and unrepresented parties, and attach the foreign subpoena; it is served and enforced under rule 1.1701, and motions to quash, enforce, or protect go to the court in the discovery county (with a $50 fee if no file exists, recoverable by the winner) and must be filed by a lawyer eligible to appear in Iowa.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.1801","t":"Computing time; holidays","s":"Time under the rules is computed under Iowa Code section 4.1(34). The rule itself sets no counting method, so the statute governs exclusion of the first day and treatment of weekends and holidays.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.1802","t":"Death, retirement or disability of judge","s":"If a presiding judge dies or becomes disabled during a proceeding or while post-trial motions are pending, another judge of the district may proceed if able, or order a continuance, mistrial, or new trial. If the judge had a bench case or motion under advisement, another district judge or one appointed by the chief justice may decide it after reviewing the transcript or reargument, or order a new trial or recall of witnesses; and if the judge dies, is disabled, or retires before the appeal record is settled, another judge settles it.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.1803","t":"Appeal to district court from administrative body","s":"When a statute allows appeal to district court from an officer, body, or board but does not provide how issues are framed, the appellant must file a petition within 10 days after perfecting the appeal (or as the court sets), the appellee must move or answer within 20 days after that (or as the court sets), and ordinary district court procedure then applies.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.1804","t":"Effect of notice by posting","s":"Notice by posting has no legal effect unless a statute expressly authorizes it.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.1805","t":"General provisions, comments and footnotes","s":"Tense, gender, and number each include the others; rule headings do not affect meaning; and source notes, comments, and footnotes are for convenience only and are not part of the rules.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.1806","t":"Rules by trial courts","s":"Each district court, by majority of its district judges, may adopt local practice and administration rules consistent with these rules, subject to the supreme court’s prior approval.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.1807","t":"Purpose of administrative rules","s":"Court administration rules exist to administer justice in an orderly, efficient, and effective manner under the highest standards of justice and judicial service.","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"},{"n":"1.1901","t":"Forms","s":"The forms in the appendix are sufficient under the rules. They are: Form 1 original notice for personal service; Form 2 original notice against a nonresident motor vehicle owner or operator under Iowa Code section 321.500; Form 3 original notice against a foreign corporation or nonresident under Iowa Code section 617.3; Form 4 original notice for publication; Form 5 directions for service; Form 6 final pretrial order; Form 7 dissolution affidavit of financial status; Forms 8, 8A, 9, and 9A financial affidavits, applications, and orders for appointment of counsel; Form 10 notice of intent to file written application for default; Form 11 petition for termination of parental rights and child support obligation; Form 12 court reporter memorandum and certificate; Forms 13 to 15 subpoenas (deposition or documents, hearing or trial, documents or inspection); Forms 16 and 17 expedited civil action certifications; Form 18 joint motion to proceed as an expedited civil action; and Form 19 health care provider statement in lieu of testimony. The form bodies themselves were not captured in the text extraction (text unclear in extraction).","u":"https://www.legis.iowa.gov/docs/ACO/CR/LINC/10-30-2026.chapter.1.pdf"}]});
